WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:08.393
- Hi everyone.

00:00:09.354 --> 00:00:12.770
I'm Priya Natarajan; I
am the current director

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:15.600
of the Franke Program in
Science and the Humanities,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.310
and I would like to give a warm welcome

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.910
to our first talk of the academic year.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.840
Our talk today is part
of the continuing series

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.500
on mapping and knowing, in
which we've been exploring

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.430
the role that maps play
across a range of disciplines.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:36.140
Mapping here in the mapping as knowing,

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.200
is construed more broadly
as delineating the boundary

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.690
between what is known from data incognita.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:47.110
All that remains to be
explored across fields.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:49.470
We've been very fortunate
that as part of this series,

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.490
we've welcomed excellent
speakers from within Yale

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:55.780
and beyond, experts across
disciplinary boundaries

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.050
who are pushing the
frontiers in their work.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:00.960
And today is no different.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.790
It is a particular pleasure, however,

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.970
to introduce one of my favorite
faculty colleagues at Yale,

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.180
Professor Brian Scassellati.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.670
He will be speaking to us
about his cutting edge work,

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.170
mapping the frontier
between man and machine.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.870
Before telling you a bit more about him

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:17.150
and his work, I would first like to thank

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.270
Mr. and Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.970
whose generous gift has
made the Franke Program

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:24.530
and this event possible.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:26.180
They are usually here,
sitting in the front row

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.100
of the audience, but they
couldn't make it today.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.460
And also I'm required
to give you this notice,

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.580
that this event is being
recorded and photographed

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.270
for educational, archival
and promotional purposes,

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.010
including use in print or on the internet,

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:40.180
and in other forms of media.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.910
By attending this event today,
you agree to the possibility

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:45.480
of your voice or likeness
captured by these means,

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.870
and used for such purposes
without compensation to you,

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:52.400
and hereby waive any related
right of privacy or publicity.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.820
So basically, you're going to,
the paparazzo are on loose.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.760
So we have warned you already.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.590
Okay, Brian Scassellati is a professor

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.470
in the departments of computer science,

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:06.720
cognitive science, and
mechanical engineering at Yale,

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.500
and he's also the director
of the NSF Expedition

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.940
on Socially Assistive Robotics.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.240
His research focuses on building embodied

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.720
computational models of
human social behavior

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.920
and interaction, especially
the developmental progression

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.750
of early social skills.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:26.220
Using computational modeling
and interactive robots,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.300
his research evaluates
models of how infants acquire

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.880
social skills and assists in the diagnosis

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.760
and quantification of disorders
of social development,

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.150
such as autism.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.900
His other interests
include humanoid robots,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.520
human-robot interaction,
artificial intelligence,

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.080
machine perception and social learning.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.350
He received his PHD in
computer science from MIT,

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:50.440
his dissertation work
was: On the Foundations

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:54.120
of the Theory of Mind
for a Humanoid Robot.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.160
Really incredibly amazing title,

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.580
with Rodney Brooks,
which used models drawn

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.870
from developmental psychology,
to build a primitive system

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.140
for allowing robots to understand people.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.540
So his work has been
deeply interdisciplinary

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.283
from the very start, combining technology

00:03:09.283 --> 00:03:11.390
in terms of computer science,

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.450
as well as developmental psychology.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:16.650
His work at MIT focused
on two very well-known

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.830
humanoid robots that you
might all have heard of,

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.210
Cog and Kismet.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:24.470
His masters degree, he was
basically a lifer at MIT,

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:27.780
his bachelor's and master's
degrees are also from there,

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.780
and he has been at Yale
since his MIT stint,

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.250
and his research in social
robotics and assistive robotics

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.120
has been recognized within
both the robotics community,

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.640
the cognitive science community,

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.100
and the much broader scientific community.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.260
He was named, I'll just
name a few of his awards.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:50.230
He has a very long list,
and I don't wanna take away

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:54.150
from his own time; he was
named an Alfred P. Sloan fellow

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.810
in 2007, won the NSF career award,

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:00.040
and his work has been awarded
five Best Paper Awards.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.930
That is pretty unusual.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:04.500
He was the chairman of
the IEEE Autonomous Mental

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.530
Development and Technical Community,

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:09.760
and program chair for the
IEEE international conference

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.320
on development and learning.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.370
In the IEEE, to be asked to chair is one

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:15.410
of the biggest honors
that you could be asked.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:18.420
So he's done that
multiple number of times.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.990
And he also chaired the
annual meeting of the

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:23.600
Cognitive Science Society in 2014.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.190
Before I hand over to Brian,
I just wanted to mention

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:29.510
that our next talk in the mapping series

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:33.110
is going to be by the Harvard
geneticist David Reich,

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:35.150
who's going to be talking
about mapping the genome,

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.110
and that's on Friday, September 27th.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:42.679
So, without further ado,
Brian, thank you very much.

00:04:42.679 --> 00:04:45.096
(applauding)

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:48.550
- Thank you all for coming out today.

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:51.730
It's wonderful to be here.

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:53.530
I proposed this title to Priya,

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:55.970
and I became enraptured with the title,

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:58.310
and it was only an hour
after that that I realized

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:01.970
I had no idea what the answer
to this was going to be.

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:03.500
So I hope you're going to join me today

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:06.340
in just thinking about
some of these really

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.570
interesting issues, about how it is

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:12.233
that we differentiate
between man and machine.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.430
Now, the first argument
I wanna put forward

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:19.030
is that really this
isn't about appearance,

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:22.330
and it isn't in fact
about reality, either.

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:26.050
That is, we're at the point
now where we're going to be

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:28.580
seeing machines that even
if they aren't compelling

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:33.470
to us today, that in the near
future, they very well may be.

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:36.730
We see robots that are
modeled as androids,

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:39.943
like this system called
Geminoid, from Hiroshi Ishiguro,

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:44.360
that maybe at first glance
looks good enough to pass,

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.630
at least a brief inspection,
but pretty quickly,

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:49.810
you can spot a few inconsistencies in it.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:51.930
You can say, oh, the
eyes aren't quite right,

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:54.920
or the way the lips curl,
or the way the wrinkles

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.493
in the skin, maybe they aren't quite it.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:02.130
And it's true that that may be the case.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.572
But we can imagine a
foreseeable future where

00:06:04.572 --> 00:06:08.100
we have systems that are so lifelike

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.493
that they are just like a person.

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.570
In fact, one of my
favorite examples of this

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:18.060
comes from a group in Europe
called Electric Circus,

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:21.400
and they have a robot named
Dirk that they routinely

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:25.340
put out in public, in various cities,

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.710
and it usually takes about
an hour or two before people

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:29.453
notice that it's a robot.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:32.300
And that's in part because it's dressed

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:35.093
like a homeless person.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:40.620
And Dirk, with its shopping
cart, wanders around the streets

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:44.753
and people for the most part
just avoid it and walk past.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:47.593
Unaware that it's a machine.

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:53.330
So, maybe it's at least
easy for us to believe,

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:56.560
at this point, that under
the right circumstances

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:59.810
or in the near future,
we will have something

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:01.300
that will look enough like a person

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:04.313
that you'll at least,
for a moment, be fooled.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.630
So if that's not the interesting
boundary, then what is?

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:14.580
Because we very much, as
humans, differentiate between

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:16.663
machines and people.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.880
We, in fact, like to draw a very firm line

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:26.390
between those things
that we treat as objects,

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:30.243
even though they might do
very intelligent things,

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:33.810
that they might be very
reasonable in the way

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:35.690
that they respond to the world,

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:37.590
that they might, in fact, even have memory

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:41.823
and learn and adapt as they
continue their existence.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.790
And there are other things
that, while they're still made

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:50.790
of the same mechanical parts,
or in fact, imaginary parts,

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:54.333
we're very happy to treat
as if they are people.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:58.973
And fundamentally, we
respond to these differently.

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:03.130
We believe that Wall-E is sad,

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:07.770
we cry when his memory is
erased, we have a feeling

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:10.357
that these robots are watching us,

00:08:10.357 --> 00:08:15.203
and that they have thoughts
and ideas, desires.

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:19.263
But we don't think about the
Voyager spacecraft in that way.

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:25.000
So why is it that we
have this clear division?

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:28.530
Why is it that we can
draw this very clean line

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:29.553
between the two?

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:33.230
Well, maybe it is just
those mechanical parts.

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:35.880
Maybe it's the fact that
the things over here

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:39.740
on the right-hand side
look more like people

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:41.610
than those things over
there on the left-hand side.

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:44.490
Maybe it's just those
anthropomorphic characteristics,

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:47.853
the presence of eyes or a face.

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:51.120
But I don't think that that's it either.

00:08:53.381 --> 00:08:56.290
Everyone's seen the
little vacuuming robots,

00:08:56.290 --> 00:09:00.960
like iRobot's Roomba, and
I think we would all be

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.750
very happy, initially, to put those over

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:07.143
on the object side of this line.

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:09.760
They don't have eyes,
they don't have a face,

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:13.260
they don't act like people at all.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:15.363
They just scurry around on the carpet.

00:09:17.639 --> 00:09:21.420
And yet, when iRobot
introduced these devices,

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:24.540
this was the first
commercial robotic system

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:28.080
that was being sold as a
consumer electronic device,

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.050
and they were worried
about people being willing

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:33.093
to buy this relatively high-priced vacuum,

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:37.990
so they put in place the most
sort of consumer-friendly

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:40.000
return policy they could.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.530
They said: if anything
happens to your Roomba,

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:44.080
you just call us, let us know,

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:46.210
we'll send you a new one right away.

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:47.980
And when you get the
new one, put the old one

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:50.703
in the box and send it back to us.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:54.683
And their customers hated this policy.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:58.020
Their customers refused to do this.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:01.570
They called and they complained,
bitterly to the company,

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:04.733
to the point that iRobot
had to change their policy,

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:09.500
because people didn't
want some strange robot

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:10.893
coming into their home.

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:15.140
They wanted their robots back.

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:18.033
Fix my robot and send it back, they said.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:21.480
Now, some of them took this even farther,

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:24.350
and they dressed up their Roombas.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:26.700
They made costumes for them.

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:28.303
They named them.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:32.430
And in fact, the way we
treat these little things

00:10:32.430 --> 00:10:33.953
that run around on the floor,

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:37.093
is often more like a person.

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:42.853
How many of you have had
a car that had a name?

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:46.840
How many of you think that your laptop

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:48.497
is sometimes out to get you?

00:10:51.371 --> 00:10:53.271
How many of you believe your cellphone

00:10:55.096 --> 00:11:00.096
not only dramatically, but
deliberately drops your calls,

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:01.653
sometimes?

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:08.590
We think about technology
in many, many ways,

00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:10.773
like it's human.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:15.540
Now, I'm not the first one
to notice this by any means,

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:20.150
and in fact, I think the two
people who made this argument

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:23.080
in the most concise and interesting way

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:25.483
were Cliff Nass and Byron Reeves.

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:29.210
And Naas and Reeves
wrote this book called:

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:30.613
The Media Equation.

00:11:31.910 --> 00:11:34.110
And they're gonna be our
starting point for talking

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:35.513
about this frontier.

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:40.410
Because their observation
was this, they said:

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:44.770
look, people evolved in a
world in which there wasn't

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:47.560
this advanced technology.

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:52.120
There were objects,
things like rocks, trees,

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:55.863
and there were agents,
things like people and dogs.

00:11:57.980 --> 00:12:01.290
And we were very clear in differentiating.

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:03.793
Rocks don't think, dogs do.

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:07.693
Trees aren't sad, humans are.

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:14.090
We made this division, but
when it came to dealing

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:18.200
with technology, we were
faced with a dilemma.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:21.950
Because we had no way of
combining those two ideas,

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:25.710
we had no intermediary category
that had half a thought,

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:27.673
or part of a feeling.

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:32.880
And so, Reeves and Nass
said when we were forced

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:36.600
to deal with these advanced
forms of technology,

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:39.200
we treated them like other agents.

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:42.433
We treated them like people or pets.

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:46.827
Now, to prove this, Reeves and Nass went

00:12:46.827 --> 00:12:50.700
and they took a few dozen experiments

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:53.630
out of psychology and social psychology

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:57.370
and they replicated them with technology.

00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:00.400
That is, they take an
experiment designed like this,

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:03.500
let's say, the guy in the beard walks in

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:06.210
and he does some service for you.

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:08.970
And then he asks: how did I do?

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:12.763
You're very likely to tell
him: good job, you did great.

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:16.190
But if the guy in the beard
comes in and he does the same

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:20.190
exact thing for you, but then
he leaves and someone else

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:22.983
walks in and says: how did
the guy in the beard do?

00:13:23.890 --> 00:13:28.130
You're more likely to give
him a negative rating.

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:30.973
You're more critical,
or maybe more honest.

00:13:33.228 --> 00:13:38.010
The same pattern holds when
we deal with technology.

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:40.182
And what Reeves and Nass did is they said:

00:13:40.182 --> 00:13:43.070
let's replace the guy in the
beard and the woman in blue

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:45.633
with two computers.

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:49.100
We'll have one computer, we'll
call it the blue computer,

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:51.380
whoops, my slides keep jumping.

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:54.000
The blue computer will come
in and give you some service,

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:56.530
and if it asks you: how did I do?

00:13:56.530 --> 00:13:59.770
You're more likely to say:
good job, blue computer.

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:01.083
You did great!

00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:05.530
But if for some reason the blue computer

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:07.760
gives you the exact same service,

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:09.220
and then we wheel it out of the room,

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:10.780
and we wheel in a red computer,

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:13.160
and the red computer says:
how'd that blue computer do?

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:16.210
You're like: well, let me tell you.

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:17.910
Here are all the problems with it.

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:22.160
That's not to say that
we rate the computer

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:24.190
as doing as good a job as the human.

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:25.800
That's not to say the
scores are identical,

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:27.943
but the same pattern holds.

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:31.527
And Reevs and Nass did this
not with one experiment,

00:14:31.527 --> 00:14:33.253
not with two experiments,

00:14:34.282 --> 00:14:37.590
but with about three dozen studies.

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:42.000
All looking at replacing
this human-human pairing

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:42.833
with a machine.

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:45.540
And in every time they did this,

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:47.523
they came up with the same result.

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:51.020
The same pattern that holds with humans

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:54.893
holds with every other kind
of technology that they used.

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:58.450
And so we call this the media equation.

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:00.750
And here's the one and only piece of math

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:03.373
that I will show today; ready, here it is.

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:08.310
It says the way we respond to humans,

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:09.817
and the way we respond to robots,

00:15:09.817 --> 00:15:11.660
and the way we respond to virtual agents,

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:14.350
and the way we respond
to any other technology

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:15.723
is basically the same.

00:15:18.410 --> 00:15:22.030
That has been our guiding
principle in looking

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:25.543
at this boundary for almost 25 years.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:29.630
Let me show you a couple
of examples of that,

00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:33.320
and some embarrassing
photos of me along the way.

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:36.170
So, Priya mentioned one
of the robots I used

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:38.220
when I was in graduate school.

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:40.880
There's me in graduate school,

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:43.260
with a robot named Cog.

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:47.683
And Cog was a big,
600-pound hulk of a robot.

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:51.160
And my job was to sit
in front of the robot

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:53.483
and build a visual system for this robot.

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:59.777
And sometimes, the robot,
even though its head was,

00:15:59.777 --> 00:16:02.450
well, not very human-like.

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:06.183
It had cameras, but it had
four cameras, not two eyes.

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:10.830
But sometimes that robot,
while I was working,

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:13.963
would turn and it would look right at me.

00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:19.520
And whenever it did this, I
would stop what I was doing,

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:20.853
and look up at the robot.

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:25.370
Now, I had written the code
that made the robot do this,

00:16:25.370 --> 00:16:27.020
and after a few seconds of looking at me,

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:29.773
it would get bored and it
would go look somewhere else.

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:34.083
And 22 seconds later, it would
come back and look at me.

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:37.890
And I would turn and look at the robot.

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:41.603
My thesis was written
in 22-second increments,

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:45.610
because for a while, I tried
for months to train myself

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:47.490
not to look at this thing,
every time it turned

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:49.520
to look to me, every
time its eyes went away,

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:50.900
I'd go back to working, and then suddenly,

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:53.400
it would look at me and
I'd stop what I was doing.

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:59.150
In the same way that if
a person had been there,

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:00.400
and they turned to look at me,

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:02.050
I would have done the same thing.

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:05.600
The pattern held the same.

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:08.580
Now sometimes that
pattern is a little harder

00:17:08.580 --> 00:17:10.173
to get and look at.

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:14.693
Let me show you another example
of that, a more recent one.

00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:20.840
In the 1950s, Solomon Asch
did a series of experiments

00:17:21.580 --> 00:17:23.793
about conformity in humans.

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:28.910
And he said: let's see what will happen

00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:32.143
if I give people a very
obvious and simple problem,

00:17:33.310 --> 00:17:37.670
but I have a bunch of
other people, confederates,

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:40.713
who give the wrong answer
right before I ask them.

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:45.620
And so he would say to them,
look over there at exhibit one,

00:17:45.620 --> 00:17:48.220
there's a line, I want you
to tell me which of these

00:17:48.220 --> 00:17:50.903
other three lines is the same length.

00:17:52.220 --> 00:17:54.080
And for the first couple of trials,

00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:58.460
his three confederates would say: A, A, A.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:01.673
And the person, the real
participant would say: A.

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:08.990
But then on the real trial,
the confederates would pick

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:11.463
a different, incorrect answer.

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:14.640
And he would ask: which one
of these is the most similar?

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:19.090
And the confederates
would all say: C, C, C!

00:18:19.090 --> 00:18:22.500
And then they would turn to
you and say: what's the answer?

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:24.393
And you knew the right answer was A.

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:28.690
But in fact, about a third of the people

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:30.683
would suddenly say: C!

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:33.743
And they'd go along with the herd.

00:18:35.390 --> 00:18:37.670
And this is a wonderful
experiment that's been replicated

00:18:37.670 --> 00:18:39.550
many times over.

00:18:39.550 --> 00:18:43.150
Now, if you try to do this
with a group of robots,

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:44.713
in fact, it doesn't work.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:51.180
If you have three robots
up there saying: C, C, C!

00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:54.850
People say: those are
some broken machines.

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:56.273
The answer is A.

00:18:57.550 --> 00:18:59.563
They don't go along with the herd.

00:19:00.440 --> 00:19:01.740
But that's because they believe

00:19:01.740 --> 00:19:04.790
that the robots might be wrong.

00:19:04.790 --> 00:19:06.530
The same sort of thing
happens if I give you

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:10.330
three children ahead of you,
and the kids all say: C, C, C!

00:19:10.330 --> 00:19:11.880
And you say: the answer is A.

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:12.713
What are you?

00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:20.880
And then all of a sudden,
you find an example of this,

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:22.033
and you have to make things

00:19:22.033 --> 00:19:23.793
just a little bit more challenging.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:27.340
So a few years ago, one
of my graduate students,

00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:32.340
Nicole Salamones, found
a way to study this.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:35.090
And it was based on a
card game named Dixit.

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:36.810
Anybody ever play this game?

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:37.920
Okay, great.

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:41.983
So in the game, there's a
bunch of very abstract cards.

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:48.860
And the person who is, who's
it for this turn will pick

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:52.793
a word and try to describe
one of these cards to you.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:59.293
And so if I say, I don't know, delicious.

00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:03.790
You would have to pick which
one of these cards do I mean.

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:07.540
Now, maybe I'm thinking about the apple

00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:09.283
in the upper left-hand corner.

00:20:10.830 --> 00:20:13.753
Or maybe I've picked up on something else.

00:20:15.290 --> 00:20:17.953
But you'd have to try to find that match.

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:21.973
Nicole did the same thing,
but with a group of robots.

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:24.840
She gave everybody a tablet.

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:29.150
Three robots, you as a
human, and another robot.

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:33.740
And the robot on the screen
in front of you said a word,

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:37.793
like lonely, and then
you were shown six cards.

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:40.660
And you indicated which of the cards

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:43.280
you thought the robot was referring to.

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:46.870
But then what she did is she said:

00:20:46.870 --> 00:20:49.230
let's reveal everyone's answers

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:51.530
and give them a chance
to change their answer.

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:56.930
So they would tell them
the two most popular

00:20:56.930 --> 00:20:59.750
selected items, and she
would either show you

00:20:59.750 --> 00:21:02.233
how many people had chosen each of them,

00:21:03.430 --> 00:21:06.323
or she would show you just
what the top two were.

00:21:09.130 --> 00:21:14.130
Now, we rigged the experiment
so that no matter which one

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:18.580
of these top two the human picked,

00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:20.933
all of the robots voted for the other one.

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:24.810
So in fact, you had to change
your preference in order

00:21:24.810 --> 00:21:26.903
to go along with the robots.

00:21:29.150 --> 00:21:32.200
But when we asked people to do this,

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:35.303
when they saw all the
robots going against them,

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:39.283
again, about a third of
them changed their answer.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:43.020
They said, well, it must not be C.

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:44.803
Or it must not be A, it must be C.

00:21:45.730 --> 00:21:47.623
Maybe all those robots are right.

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:52.050
So sometimes it takes just
a little bit more to dig

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:53.930
and find that pattern,
but for the most part,

00:21:53.930 --> 00:21:58.653
we still find that same
pattern, that media equation.

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:02.830
Humans, the way we interact with people,

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:04.800
the way we interact with
robots, the way we interact

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:06.763
with virtual agents, it's all the same.

00:22:09.190 --> 00:22:12.310
Now, if we wanna explore
this frontier, though,

00:22:12.310 --> 00:22:15.360
the most interesting parts
are gonna be the places

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:17.673
where this equation breaks.

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:21.323
Those are the little
notches in the boundary,

00:22:22.550 --> 00:22:24.973
where something is somehow different.

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:29.370
So for the rest of today, I
wanna try to show you some

00:22:29.370 --> 00:22:33.563
of those unique places
where things are different.

00:22:36.040 --> 00:22:38.810
Now, that's not to say that we,

00:22:38.810 --> 00:22:41.140
do we treat robots differently
than we treat humans?

00:22:41.140 --> 00:22:42.363
Yes, of course we do.

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:45.890
But where the patterns change,

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:48.040
that's where things
get really interesting.

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:51.943
Okay, so let's consider
just this first part.

00:22:53.140 --> 00:22:56.610
When is it that we treat
people and robots differently?

00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:58.370
Let's forget about other
forms of technology,

00:22:58.370 --> 00:23:00.120
and let's just deal with these two.

00:23:02.210 --> 00:23:06.630
Let me introduce, first, a
little robot named Keepon,

00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:09.100
that you saw the caricature of before.

00:23:09.100 --> 00:23:11.080
And here's what the robot
looks like in real life.

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:13.493
It's only about three or four inches tall,

00:23:14.750 --> 00:23:17.470
and it's moderately expressive.

00:23:17.470 --> 00:23:19.280
You can tell what direction it's looking,

00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:20.730
it can sort of bounce up and down

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:22.673
to express itself a little bit,

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:24.793
but not much more.

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:28.963
It's minimally anthropomorphic.

00:23:30.740 --> 00:23:33.900
We brought this robot into
classrooms, here in New Haven.

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:36.090
There's a wonderful Magnet
School here in New Haven

00:23:36.090 --> 00:23:39.250
where about 90 percent
of the kids speak Spanish

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:41.900
or Portuguese at home,
and they're struggling

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:44.850
to learn English in the
public school system.

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:48.670
And we went in and we talked
to classroom teachers.

00:23:48.670 --> 00:23:52.390
Not the administrators, the
actual K through two teachers.

00:23:52.390 --> 00:23:53.223
And we said to them:

00:23:53.223 --> 00:23:55.343
what's the biggest problem that you have?

00:23:56.560 --> 00:23:59.530
And every one of these teachers
said the same thing to us.

00:23:59.530 --> 00:24:01.113
Anybody wanna guess what it was?

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:05.200
What do you think these
teachers said was difficult?

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:08.500
Attention span.

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:09.610
Nah, they're kindergarten teachers.

00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:12.460
They're used to dealing with
kids with no attention span.

00:24:15.280 --> 00:24:17.341
What do you think it was?

00:24:17.341 --> 00:24:19.953
(muttering)

00:24:19.953 --> 00:24:22.350
Limited number of students
per teacher, or you know,

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:24.180
not enough time to go one on one.

00:24:24.180 --> 00:24:25.670
That's a lot of it.

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:27.180
But you know what they said, you know,

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:29.560
even if we didn't have that,
even with just the resources

00:24:29.560 --> 00:24:34.103
we have, we could be doing
better, except for one thing.

00:24:37.163 --> 00:24:39.397
And it's exactly what's
happening right now.

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:45.273
The teacher would ask a
question, and no one speaks up.

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:48.560
And so they would pick on people.

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:50.660
They would ask a really, an easy question,

00:24:51.700 --> 00:24:54.350
and they would ask one of the students,

00:24:54.350 --> 00:24:57.230
and the students would deflect.

00:24:57.230 --> 00:24:58.403
They'd avoid answering.

00:25:00.790 --> 00:25:05.000
Because they were afraid,
they were embarrassed,

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:07.030
they didn't want to say the wrong thing

00:25:07.030 --> 00:25:09.290
in front of the teacher and
in front of their classmates,

00:25:09.290 --> 00:25:12.500
they were worried that they
would say it in the wrong way,

00:25:12.500 --> 00:25:14.800
in this language that
they were just learning.

00:25:16.170 --> 00:25:17.680
And so the teachers said to us, they said:

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:20.220
our number one problem is we have no idea

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:22.483
what our kids know and
what they don't know.

00:25:25.750 --> 00:25:27.160
And we said: well, what would happen

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:28.603
if you had one on one time?

00:25:29.450 --> 00:25:31.320
What would happen if you
could sit down one on one?

00:25:31.320 --> 00:25:33.430
Would that solve this problem?

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:34.940
And they said, for the most part, no.

00:25:34.940 --> 00:25:38.603
Because the kids still don't
wanna tell us the wrong answer.

00:25:40.780 --> 00:25:43.033
So we brought in robots like this,

00:25:44.114 --> 00:25:48.750
and we sat them down,
and these kids suddenly

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:52.453
were willing to make mistakes
in front of the robot.

00:25:53.570 --> 00:25:56.930
Here's a child who's
being asked to translate

00:25:56.930 --> 00:25:59.290
the Spanish verb "hacer" into English.

00:25:59.290 --> 00:26:02.110
Now, hacer in English
either becomes make or do,

00:26:02.110 --> 00:26:05.830
depending on about 14
different context rules.

00:26:05.830 --> 00:26:08.300
You make your bed, you don't do your bed.

00:26:08.300 --> 00:26:10.913
You do your homework, you
don't make your homework.

00:26:13.410 --> 00:26:16.733
And learning those requires
learning those context rules.

00:26:18.660 --> 00:26:22.120
You're gonna see him make a mistake.

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:23.970
The robot's going to ask him, in Spanish,

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:26.677
to translate something into English,

00:26:26.677 --> 00:26:28.177
and he's gonna make a mistake.

00:26:35.546 --> 00:26:37.713
- You can do a space more.

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:54.463
- So instead of make more
space, he says: do a space more.

00:26:55.930 --> 00:26:56.923
He gets it wrong.

00:26:58.030 --> 00:27:01.180
And in fact, the robot recognizes this.

00:27:01.180 --> 00:27:03.880
It recognizes which of
these 14 context rules

00:27:03.880 --> 00:27:07.360
he has trouble with, and
it can give him more help

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:08.763
on that one in particular.

00:27:09.830 --> 00:27:13.380
And so when kids sit
down with these robots,

00:27:13.380 --> 00:27:17.740
we get kids who, in five,
15-minute sessions with the robot,

00:27:17.740 --> 00:27:21.460
go from 30 percentile
on standardized tests

00:27:21.460 --> 00:27:24.653
to 80 percentile on standardized
tests on that material.

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:31.053
All because we can target what
exactly they need help with.

00:27:32.210 --> 00:27:34.700
We can't do this with the
same amount of classroom

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:36.380
instruction, we can't do
this with the same amount

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:39.000
of one on one instruction from a teacher,

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:41.383
because here the pattern is different.

00:27:43.930 --> 00:27:45.720
With adults, with other kids,

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:49.240
they're too embarrassed
to make these mistakes.

00:27:49.240 --> 00:27:52.393
But they're okay making these
mistakes with the robot.

00:27:55.320 --> 00:27:57.230
Let me show you another
example of how robots

00:27:57.230 --> 00:27:58.530
are different from people.

00:27:59.600 --> 00:28:03.090
This is a robot that we
use in clinical practice,

00:28:03.090 --> 00:28:07.630
here at the Child Studies Center
at Yale New Haven Hospital.

00:28:07.630 --> 00:28:09.130
And it's a robot called Ellie.

00:28:10.250 --> 00:28:11.850
And we work with kids with autism,

00:28:11.850 --> 00:28:14.523
kids who have difficulty
with social skills,

00:28:15.400 --> 00:28:17.090
and one of the things
that's really difficult

00:28:17.090 --> 00:28:21.540
when you do therapy is
giving those clients

00:28:21.540 --> 00:28:24.323
a sense that they have
mastered the material.

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:29.660
Because so much of their
therapy is basically repetition,

00:28:29.660 --> 00:28:32.430
over and over and over again,
of nope, that was wrong.

00:28:32.430 --> 00:28:33.263
Do it again.

00:28:33.263 --> 00:28:34.863
Nope, that was wrong; do it again.

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:38.750
So we bring in Ellie,

00:28:38.750 --> 00:28:42.410
and we've programmed
Ellie to make mistakes.

00:28:42.410 --> 00:28:45.100
In fact, to make the same
mistakes that these kids

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:46.143
had practiced on.

00:28:47.740 --> 00:28:50.043
Ellie interrupts them
while they're talking,

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:54.640
Ellie looks away from them
when its talking to them.

00:28:54.640 --> 00:28:57.823
Ellie introduces off-topic conversations.

00:29:00.100 --> 00:29:03.743
And we ask the kids to become teachers,

00:29:04.640 --> 00:29:06.333
and to help teach Ellie.

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:13.123
And when we do that, they're
thoroughly happy to do so.

00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:18.370
Much more so than when we
do this with a human adult.

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:20.077
So you're going to see
the same child on the top

00:29:20.077 --> 00:29:22.740
and the bottom, but on the top,

00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:25.770
we've told him that Laura,
the very nice blond woman

00:29:25.770 --> 00:29:29.390
on the left-hand side, who he's never met,

00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:32.960
needs this help, and he's
supposed to teach her.

00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:35.700
And on the bottom, we've
told him the robot, Ellie,

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:36.783
needs that help.

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:40.270
And when he comes into the
clinic for the first time,

00:29:40.270 --> 00:29:44.210
and he sits with these two,
he's thoroughly excited

00:29:44.210 --> 00:29:47.643
to help Ellie, and right
away, he gets started.

00:29:49.060 --> 00:29:52.060
And when he does this
with Laura on the top,

00:29:52.060 --> 00:29:54.823
he has no interest in engaging with her.

00:29:55.960 --> 00:29:58.463
And in fact, he mostly
just stares in the mirror.

00:30:00.450 --> 00:30:04.273
He doesn't believe that Laura
really needs help like this.

00:30:05.141 --> 00:30:08.780
He doesn't believe that that's
a role that she can take on.

00:30:08.780 --> 00:30:12.240
But he believes it of the
robot, because we don't have

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:15.430
predefined ideas about what
the robot is supposed to be.

00:30:15.430 --> 00:30:17.690
And so if I tell you
this robot needs help,

00:30:17.690 --> 00:30:19.283
well, of course it needs help.

00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:24.880
If I tell you it has the same
problems that you used to,

00:30:24.880 --> 00:30:27.113
well okay, I could believe that.

00:30:29.700 --> 00:30:31.610
The robots work in these situations,

00:30:31.610 --> 00:30:34.880
because they can take on these roles

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:36.843
that we can't easily put adults in.

00:30:39.370 --> 00:30:41.530
This is one of our best
therapies, actually,

00:30:41.530 --> 00:30:45.113
because the kids walk away
from this feeling great.

00:30:46.100 --> 00:30:49.653
They feel like they actually
are masters of the material.

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:54.784
So when we look at these
two, humans and robots,

00:30:54.784 --> 00:30:57.710
there are a few things
that break our pattern.

00:30:57.710 --> 00:30:59.810
The robots are social,
and they're social enough

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:03.180
that we look them in the
eye and we respond to them,

00:31:03.180 --> 00:31:04.623
but they're not too social.

00:31:05.610 --> 00:31:07.040
They don't trigger embarrassment,

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:09.523
they don't make us self conscious.

00:31:10.790 --> 00:31:14.240
And we don't have
predefined roles for them.

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:17.383
We're willing to believe
whatever we're told with them.

00:31:20.070 --> 00:31:22.413
Okay, how about the other
side of this equation?

00:31:23.810 --> 00:31:27.150
Maybe people are different from robots,

00:31:27.150 --> 00:31:28.900
but then maybe all of the different types

00:31:28.900 --> 00:31:30.580
of technology, maybe they're all the same,

00:31:30.580 --> 00:31:33.403
maybe we respond to them in the same ways.

00:31:34.240 --> 00:31:35.930
I could believe that if
I have a different way

00:31:35.930 --> 00:31:38.760
of treating technology,
then maybe I treat all

00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:43.013
of the technologies with
this same strange pattern.

00:31:45.830 --> 00:31:47.560
Let me show you a couple, again,

00:31:47.560 --> 00:31:49.223
things that break this rule.

00:31:51.440 --> 00:31:55.500
These are both wonderful
experiments that were both proposed

00:31:55.500 --> 00:31:57.670
by Yale undergraduates.

00:31:57.670 --> 00:32:00.120
So I get to say that as a special mark

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:02.260
of distinction for them.

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:07.180
The first one was an
experiment that was designed

00:32:07.180 --> 00:32:10.913
by Wilma Bainbridge,
when she was a sophomore.

00:32:12.050 --> 00:32:13.910
And when she was a sophomore, she came in,

00:32:13.910 --> 00:32:16.340
and I'd had her in class,
and she said to me:

00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:18.650
I have this great idea,
but I need some things

00:32:18.650 --> 00:32:20.823
in order to do this experiment.

00:32:21.853 --> 00:32:22.920
And I said: okay, what do you need?

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:24.540
Tell me about it, what
do you need, you know?

00:32:24.540 --> 00:32:26.900
Figuring she's gonna tell
me a certain amount of money

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:30.200
to pay participants or
maybe some equipment,

00:32:30.200 --> 00:32:31.120
and she got right to the point.

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:34.103
She said: no, I need your office.

00:32:36.420 --> 00:32:39.490
And by the time she'd finished
explaining to me, I said:

00:32:39.490 --> 00:32:41.070
this sounds great.

00:32:41.070 --> 00:32:44.880
And I turned over my office
to Wilma for three months.

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:49.243
I hid out in the library and
in my lab for three months.

00:32:50.300 --> 00:32:52.283
And while I was gone and hiding away,

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:56.143
Wilma went to all of her
friends and she said:

00:32:57.070 --> 00:32:59.370
look, I'm the lab manager
for this professor,

00:32:59.370 --> 00:33:01.263
and he's on sabbatical.

00:33:02.730 --> 00:33:05.690
He's given me all this work to do,

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:07.180
and I don't have time for it,

00:33:07.180 --> 00:33:08.900
but he told me I could hire people.

00:33:08.900 --> 00:33:12.570
Would you come do an hour
worth of clerical tasks?

00:33:12.570 --> 00:33:14.523
I'll pay you 30 bucks.

00:33:16.750 --> 00:33:20.820
And she got about 100
undergraduates to say yes.

00:33:20.820 --> 00:33:22.580
And so she brought them
all into my office,

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:26.320
what was clearly a
faculty office on campus,

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:28.610
and when they walked in the room,

00:33:28.610 --> 00:33:32.720
half of them walked in
and sat down next to this

00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:35.433
little, two and a half foot tall robot.

00:33:36.710 --> 00:33:38.730
The other half of them
walked in and sat down

00:33:38.730 --> 00:33:43.730
next to a flat screen showing
a two-way video conference,

00:33:45.260 --> 00:33:46.633
with the same robot.

00:33:47.980 --> 00:33:49.890
Now, whether they sat
next to the real robot,

00:33:49.890 --> 00:33:54.570
or the robot on the screen,
both robots did the same things.

00:33:54.570 --> 00:33:56.360
The robots would wave
to them when they walked

00:33:56.360 --> 00:33:59.560
into the room, would
follow them and watch them

00:33:59.560 --> 00:34:02.140
as they walked around the room,

00:34:02.140 --> 00:34:06.763
would occasionally try to
make a silly gesture to them,

00:34:07.610 --> 00:34:10.000
and mostly acted bored.

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:12.190
And while they were there,
getting used the robots,

00:34:12.190 --> 00:34:14.300
we gave them some office tasks.

00:34:14.300 --> 00:34:17.380
File some documents, proofread this,

00:34:17.380 --> 00:34:19.183
alphabetize these exams.

00:34:21.410 --> 00:34:25.000
And then the, Wilma sent them
an instant message and said:

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:26.940
we've got one last job for you to do

00:34:26.940 --> 00:34:28.910
before the end of the hour.

00:34:28.910 --> 00:34:30.650
There's some books that
are all over the place

00:34:30.650 --> 00:34:32.030
in the professor's office.

00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:34.040
We need them put away.

00:34:34.040 --> 00:34:37.033
The robot knows where the books belong.

00:34:39.460 --> 00:34:43.350
And suddenly the robot, who'd
been completely distracted,

00:34:43.350 --> 00:34:48.193
paid attention and started
pointing to piles of books.

00:34:49.320 --> 00:34:52.140
And everybody did what the robot asked.

00:34:52.140 --> 00:34:53.880
They went over and they
picked put the books,

00:34:53.880 --> 00:34:55.520
and then the robot would
point to a location

00:34:55.520 --> 00:34:57.860
on the shelf, and they'd
go and they'd put the books

00:34:57.860 --> 00:34:58.803
on the shelf.

00:35:00.370 --> 00:35:02.463
Three, four times they'd put books away.

00:35:03.310 --> 00:35:08.130
And then finally, the
robot points to this nice,

00:35:08.130 --> 00:35:12.530
new pile of textbooks on
the corner of my desk.

00:35:12.530 --> 00:35:15.363
Five, $600 worth of textbooks.

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:19.173
And then it points to the trashcan.

00:35:20.620 --> 00:35:24.280
And the question was: how
many Yale undergraduates,

00:35:24.280 --> 00:35:27.710
sitting in what was
clearly a faculty office,

00:35:27.710 --> 00:35:31.810
would throw away $600 worth
of brand new textbooks

00:35:31.810 --> 00:35:34.213
because the robot said so?

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:38.260
Anyone want to guess?

00:35:38.260 --> 00:35:41.270
How many of you would have
thrown the books away?

00:35:41.270 --> 00:35:43.070
I would have thrown them away, yeah.

00:35:44.350 --> 00:35:45.183
Right?

00:35:45.183 --> 00:35:48.033
We all like to think maybe
one or two of us would do it.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:52.040
In fact, when it was the real robot,

00:35:52.040 --> 00:35:54.820
when people sat next to the real robot,

00:35:54.820 --> 00:35:57.200
two-thirds of them picked up the books,

00:35:57.200 --> 00:35:59.140
threw them in the trashcan, turned around

00:35:59.140 --> 00:36:02.753
for their next job, with
no hesitation whatsoever.

00:36:04.910 --> 00:36:07.650
No questioning, no nothing,
just picked the books,

00:36:07.650 --> 00:36:09.260
threw them in the trash, (muttering).

00:36:10.880 --> 00:36:12.853
What's next; I want my 30 bucks.

00:36:13.697 --> 00:36:17.423
When it was the robot on the screen,

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:22.513
only about 17 percent
of them threw them away.

00:36:23.880 --> 00:36:25.690
They all picked up the books.

00:36:25.690 --> 00:36:27.780
They all walked over to the trashcan,

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:30.033
they were clear on what the robot wanted.

00:36:31.010 --> 00:36:33.723
Most of them tried to
negotiate with the robot.

00:36:35.100 --> 00:36:37.160
Are you sure you want, they would mime

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:38.600
throwing the books away to see if they

00:36:38.600 --> 00:36:40.423
could get the robot to respond.

00:36:41.690 --> 00:36:43.860
They eventually usually hid the books

00:36:43.860 --> 00:36:45.863
on a shelf near the trashcan.

00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:49.223
Or behind the trashcan.

00:36:50.430 --> 00:36:52.640
One genius undergraduate
put the books on the floor,

00:36:52.640 --> 00:36:54.973
put the trashcan on top of the books.

00:36:56.060 --> 00:36:58.463
Anything to avoid throwing them away.

00:37:01.460 --> 00:37:03.360
Now, we thought maybe this
was an effect that they just

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:05.580
really didn't understand,
and maybe the flat screen

00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:07.950
they were losing something,
so we ran a third condition

00:37:07.950 --> 00:37:09.960
of this where not only
did you see the robot

00:37:09.960 --> 00:37:11.430
on the flat screen, but there was

00:37:11.430 --> 00:37:14.810
another monitor next to it,
and on the monitor next to it,

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:16.440
there was a picture of the trashcan,

00:37:16.440 --> 00:37:18.643
and an arrow going into the trashcan.

00:37:21.090 --> 00:37:23.723
Still, less than 20 percent
of them threw it away.

00:37:25.200 --> 00:37:27.523
But when it was the real robot there,

00:37:28.420 --> 00:37:30.970
two thirds of them did it
without a second thought.

00:37:32.140 --> 00:37:33.472
We asked them afterwards.

00:37:33.472 --> 00:37:36.450
They didn't even think
what they did was unusual.

00:37:36.450 --> 00:37:38.650
The robot said so; of
course I should do it.

00:37:39.700 --> 00:37:41.033
Not a second thought.

00:37:44.060 --> 00:37:46.183
Why are those two technologies different?

00:37:48.290 --> 00:37:52.140
Well, in fact, we know these answers.

00:37:52.140 --> 00:37:55.440
That when we have a physical presence,

00:37:55.440 --> 00:37:57.893
that changes the way we respond.

00:37:59.240 --> 00:38:01.070
We knew this about ourselves long before

00:38:01.070 --> 00:38:02.430
we knew this about robots.

00:38:02.430 --> 00:38:05.150
You know why we had door to door salesmen?

00:38:05.150 --> 00:38:06.310
Because when you've got someone there,

00:38:06.310 --> 00:38:09.210
standing at your door, it's
really hard to say no to them.

00:38:11.090 --> 00:38:13.200
You go to business school,
you're taught in business school

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:15.550
you've got a big deal, you
wanna close the big deal,

00:38:15.550 --> 00:38:18.370
you don't email, you don't
Skype, you don't write,

00:38:18.370 --> 00:38:21.623
you go there in person to
get the big deal signed.

00:38:22.830 --> 00:38:25.860
Because people are more
likely to do what you want

00:38:25.860 --> 00:38:26.913
when you're there.

00:38:28.920 --> 00:38:33.293
Same is true with robots, but
not with things on a screen.

00:38:35.420 --> 00:38:37.730
When the real robot says to
you: throw those books away.

00:38:37.730 --> 00:38:39.643
You go: yup, throw those books away.

00:38:40.840 --> 00:38:43.263
In psychology, we call this compliance.

00:38:46.550 --> 00:38:49.943
So robots and other technology
might not be the same.

00:38:51.250 --> 00:38:53.650
Let me give you one more
reason to believe that.

00:38:56.113 --> 00:38:59.440
We had a group of undergraduates
who wanted to do this,

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:02.830
but with a really cognitive puzzle.

00:39:02.830 --> 00:39:04.930
Any of you ever seen these puzzles before?

00:39:06.130 --> 00:39:08.010
Anybody know what they are?

00:39:08.010 --> 00:39:09.343
Maybe a few, okay.

00:39:10.240 --> 00:39:12.500
These are called nonograms or pick across.

00:39:12.500 --> 00:39:15.160
How many of you have ever played Sudoku?

00:39:15.160 --> 00:39:16.970
Okay, it's similar to Sudoku.

00:39:16.970 --> 00:39:19.600
It's kind of a little logic puzzle.

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:21.130
And the way the puzzle works is you start

00:39:21.130 --> 00:39:23.780
with a blank puzzle, and at the end,

00:39:23.780 --> 00:39:26.420
you finish the puzzle by
shading in some of the squares,

00:39:26.420 --> 00:39:27.720
but not all of them.

00:39:27.720 --> 00:39:29.930
And which squares you
shade in are determined

00:39:29.930 --> 00:39:33.700
by the numbers on the
rows and the columns.

00:39:33.700 --> 00:39:35.260
So if you look at that bottom row,

00:39:35.260 --> 00:39:37.447
it has the numbers six and then one.

00:39:38.290 --> 00:39:41.190
That means on that row,
you've gotta have six squares

00:39:41.190 --> 00:39:45.530
together that are contiguous, shaded in.

00:39:45.530 --> 00:39:47.310
And then some number of blank squares,

00:39:47.310 --> 00:39:50.090
and then one shaded in square.

00:39:50.090 --> 00:39:52.540
And you can have blanks
before it or blanks after it,

00:39:52.540 --> 00:39:55.023
but it's gotta look like six and then one.

00:39:56.640 --> 00:40:00.550
Now, on that bottom row, there's
only eight squares across.

00:40:00.550 --> 00:40:03.380
There's only one way for you to do that.

00:40:03.380 --> 00:40:06.610
But if you look a the row
above it, it says seven.

00:40:06.610 --> 00:40:08.640
You don't know whether
it's the first seven

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:11.463
or the last seven that
you're supposed to shade in,

00:40:12.830 --> 00:40:15.320
and you won't know that for
sure until you start looking

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:18.440
at the constraints on the columns.

00:40:18.440 --> 00:40:19.850
Because not only do you have
to get all the rows right,

00:40:19.850 --> 00:40:22.303
you have to get all the
columns right, as well.

00:40:23.720 --> 00:40:24.610
In computer science,

00:40:24.610 --> 00:40:26.670
we call this a constraint
satisfaction problem.

00:40:26.670 --> 00:40:28.250
It's NP hard.

00:40:28.250 --> 00:40:29.740
It's kind of the hardest kind of puzzles

00:40:29.740 --> 00:40:31.763
we like to deal with computationally.

00:40:33.620 --> 00:40:36.260
For those of you who
aren't computer scientists,

00:40:36.260 --> 00:40:39.910
think of it this way: a
10 by 10 puzzle like this

00:40:39.910 --> 00:40:42.980
would take you about 15 minutes to solve.

00:40:42.980 --> 00:40:44.080
They're pretty tricky.

00:40:46.170 --> 00:40:49.300
Now, that's not to say that
I couldn't help you out

00:40:49.300 --> 00:40:51.650
a little bit, because
even though you now know

00:40:51.650 --> 00:40:55.263
all the rules to do this, I
could give you some hints.

00:40:56.810 --> 00:40:59.190
For example, I could teach you this hint.

00:40:59.190 --> 00:41:03.163
I could say: look, in that row of seven,

00:41:04.380 --> 00:41:05.980
if I don't know whether
it's the first seven

00:41:05.980 --> 00:41:08.270
or the last seven, there's still something

00:41:08.270 --> 00:41:09.843
that I know right away.

00:41:10.990 --> 00:41:12.333
What can I say for sure?

00:41:15.670 --> 00:41:18.500
The middle six, right?

00:41:18.500 --> 00:41:20.880
The middle six have to be shaded in.

00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:23.630
So as soon as I see that
seven, I could go and do that.

00:41:24.530 --> 00:41:28.360
Okay, we came up with a
set of about 20 hints,

00:41:28.360 --> 00:41:30.233
or lessons like these.

00:41:31.140 --> 00:41:34.340
And then we brought in a
few hundred undergraduates,

00:41:34.340 --> 00:41:37.620
and we sat them down and we
broke them into four groups.

00:41:37.620 --> 00:41:40.710
One group got taught the
rules, just like you did.

00:41:40.710 --> 00:41:44.640
And then left on their
own, as a control group.

00:41:44.640 --> 00:41:46.790
Just: how well can you
do without any help?

00:41:47.870 --> 00:41:52.120
The second and the third
group got the same hints,

00:41:52.120 --> 00:41:56.270
but either delivered from
just the speakers of a laptop,

00:41:56.270 --> 00:41:58.923
or from a little video
of that Keepon robot.

00:42:00.590 --> 00:42:04.380
And the last group got
the same recorded hints,

00:42:04.380 --> 00:42:07.230
but they got a real, physical
robot sitting next to them.

00:42:08.320 --> 00:42:10.280
Now, we played one last
dirty trick on them,

00:42:10.280 --> 00:42:13.360
which is we had them solve three puzzles,

00:42:13.360 --> 00:42:16.780
but the first and the last
puzzle were actually the same.

00:42:16.780 --> 00:42:19.193
Just rotated by 90 degrees.

00:42:20.490 --> 00:42:23.340
That meant they were just exactly as hard,

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:25.890
but no one ever recognized
them as the same puzzle.

00:42:27.310 --> 00:42:30.020
So we could compare how fast
they solved it the first time

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:32.220
and how fast they solved
it the second time.

00:42:33.300 --> 00:42:35.490
Now, if you were in that
first group who got no help

00:42:35.490 --> 00:42:38.070
whatsoever, just got taught the rules,

00:42:38.070 --> 00:42:40.360
you improve your performance
by about two minutes.

00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:43.113
You go from 15 minutes to
13 minutes solving time.

00:42:44.110 --> 00:42:46.073
Just practice makes you better.

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:50.030
If you got some of those hints,

00:42:50.030 --> 00:42:55.030
either from that disembodied
laptop voice or from the video

00:42:56.210 --> 00:42:59.290
of the robot, you improved
by about three and a half,

00:42:59.290 --> 00:43:00.123
four minutes.

00:43:00.123 --> 00:43:01.283
Those hints were useful.

00:43:02.690 --> 00:43:05.760
But if you got the same
hints, but delivered

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:10.760
from the robot, you improved
by about six minutes or more.

00:43:12.520 --> 00:43:14.180
There's no good reason for this.

00:43:14.180 --> 00:43:15.930
You're not getting any extra help.

00:43:15.930 --> 00:43:18.830
It's not like the presence of
that nice, little yellow robot

00:43:18.830 --> 00:43:20.643
suddenly makes you smarter.

00:43:21.820 --> 00:43:23.780
It's not giving you any more information

00:43:23.780 --> 00:43:25.563
than what you had from the video.

00:43:28.400 --> 00:43:31.280
But in fact, you learn better
when there's someone there

00:43:31.280 --> 00:43:36.280
in person; this says go to class.

00:43:38.420 --> 00:43:39.950
Just show up for lecture.

00:43:39.950 --> 00:43:43.283
You will learn more than
watching the video later online.

00:43:45.930 --> 00:43:48.240
And in fact, if you don't
believe these two studies

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:50.910
that were run here by undergraduates,

00:43:50.910 --> 00:43:54.540
you should realize that each
of them have been replicated

00:43:54.540 --> 00:43:58.180
about 40 times, and there
was a review in Science

00:43:58.180 --> 00:44:01.730
last summer, and in all of
those different studies,

00:44:01.730 --> 00:44:05.103
the same size effect basically occurs.

00:44:06.010 --> 00:44:09.730
People are more compliant
with that physical robot,

00:44:09.730 --> 00:44:12.543
and they learn more from
that physical system.

00:44:15.440 --> 00:44:16.273
Okay.

00:44:17.790 --> 00:44:19.550
There's one more part to this equation

00:44:19.550 --> 00:44:20.650
that we wanna look at.

00:44:21.680 --> 00:44:26.070
Maybe it's just that robots are special.

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:27.110
Now, I'm a roboticist.

00:44:27.110 --> 00:44:29.283
I just wanna believe that, from the start.

00:44:30.490 --> 00:44:33.700
Maybe it's that robots, oops,
again, I'm jumping ahead

00:44:33.700 --> 00:44:38.490
too quickly, maybe robots
just are different.

00:44:38.490 --> 00:44:40.360
They're in some ways like people,

00:44:40.360 --> 00:44:42.403
in some ways like technology.

00:44:43.530 --> 00:44:46.430
Maybe there's some things that
are just unique about that.

00:44:48.410 --> 00:44:51.383
All right, let me pose you a challenge.

00:44:52.260 --> 00:44:56.690
When I was in grad school,
I had another robot,

00:44:56.690 --> 00:44:58.323
and this was a robot called Kismet.

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:01.560
And this thing which was
only about a foot high,

00:45:02.960 --> 00:45:06.260
had these different facial expressions.

00:45:06.260 --> 00:45:08.810
It had some anthropomorphic
characteristics.

00:45:08.810 --> 00:45:11.190
It had eyes and a mouth and lips,

00:45:11.190 --> 00:45:13.823
and I also had these
kind of dog-like ears.

00:45:16.600 --> 00:45:19.630
And it could make this wide
variety of expressions,

00:45:19.630 --> 00:45:22.323
some of which were more or less friendly.

00:45:24.800 --> 00:45:28.330
All right, so let's go back
to kind of a friendly version

00:45:28.330 --> 00:45:31.983
of the robot here, and
let's pause it right there.

00:45:33.390 --> 00:45:35.980
Because when this robot
was operating in the lab,

00:45:35.980 --> 00:45:38.483
most of the time it was
a pretty happy robot,

00:45:39.650 --> 00:45:42.520
and we brought in, my advisor loved

00:45:42.520 --> 00:45:45.110
bringing in school groups for tours,

00:45:45.110 --> 00:45:46.907
and we brought in probably about

00:45:46.907 --> 00:45:50.300
1,000 kids to see this robot.

00:45:50.300 --> 00:45:52.417
And they all saw the robot,
and they all ran over to it,

00:45:52.417 --> 00:45:55.550
and the robot would
babble randomly to them.

00:45:55.550 --> 00:45:56.840
And they were excited, they'd go over,

00:45:56.840 --> 00:45:58.030
and they'd see what the robot did.

00:45:58.030 --> 00:46:00.200
They tried testing out different things,

00:46:00.200 --> 00:46:02.860
they'd play with the robot
to see what it could do,

00:46:02.860 --> 00:46:05.143
but all of them were
happy to engage with it.

00:46:06.990 --> 00:46:08.293
Except for one group.

00:46:10.470 --> 00:46:15.470
At one summer, we had a group
of about 20 foreign students.

00:46:17.720 --> 00:46:22.720
A group of about 13 to 14
year old girls from Japan

00:46:23.040 --> 00:46:27.513
who had come to Boston
and came to tour the lab.

00:46:28.820 --> 00:46:31.090
And we brought them in to see this robot,

00:46:31.090 --> 00:46:34.010
and the robot smiled at them,
and it wiggled its ears,

00:46:34.010 --> 00:46:35.543
and it babbled at them.

00:46:36.890 --> 00:46:38.480
And every single one of those girls

00:46:38.480 --> 00:46:40.083
backed up against the far wall.

00:46:42.050 --> 00:46:45.700
And they wanted nothing
to do with this robot.

00:46:45.700 --> 00:46:48.310
We thought maybe that
this was about permission,

00:46:48.310 --> 00:46:50.050
and being, you know, permitted to go.

00:46:50.050 --> 00:46:51.900
And we said: no, no, no,
you can come up close.

00:46:51.900 --> 00:46:52.827
And we demonstrated it to them,

00:46:52.827 --> 00:46:54.903
and we tried to bring them up close,

00:46:55.800 --> 00:46:56.980
and none of them wanted to.

00:46:56.980 --> 00:46:59.473
They were scared of this robot.

00:47:01.446 --> 00:47:02.740
And it took us a long time to figure out,

00:47:02.740 --> 00:47:06.400
but in fact, this robot is
missing one very important

00:47:06.400 --> 00:47:09.863
thing in its face that
made them feel this way.

00:47:12.060 --> 00:47:13.060
What was that thing?

00:47:13.060 --> 00:47:14.393
What's the robot missing?

00:47:15.630 --> 00:47:17.794
A nose; nope, that's not it.

00:47:17.794 --> 00:47:18.690
- [Attendee] Teeth.

00:47:18.690 --> 00:47:19.810
- Teeth; nope, that's not it.

00:47:19.810 --> 00:47:21.420
Keep 'em coming.

00:47:21.420 --> 00:47:22.453
Hair, that's not it.

00:47:24.700 --> 00:47:28.110
A face itself; nope, they're
perfectly happy without that.

00:47:28.110 --> 00:47:29.003
What else?

00:47:30.141 --> 00:47:30.974
A chin, nope.

00:47:32.090 --> 00:47:33.243
Tongue, nope.

00:47:35.290 --> 00:47:37.750
It's missing all these pieces, true.

00:47:37.750 --> 00:47:39.324
But this was not it.

00:47:39.324 --> 00:47:40.157
- [Attendee] Eyelashes?

00:47:40.157 --> 00:47:41.883
- Eyelashes, no, it has eyelashes.

00:47:43.000 --> 00:47:43.833
A voice?

00:47:43.833 --> 00:47:47.250
Nope, it had a voice;
it was talking to them.

00:47:47.250 --> 00:47:50.739
Hair, nope, they're perfectly
happy with bald robots.

00:47:50.739 --> 00:47:52.400
(muttering)

00:47:52.400 --> 00:47:53.471
What is it?

00:47:53.471 --> 00:47:54.304
(muttering)

00:47:54.304 --> 00:47:55.137
Skin, nope.

00:47:55.137 --> 00:47:57.203
Skinless robots are, forehead, nope.

00:47:59.069 --> 00:48:02.120
(muttering)

00:48:02.120 --> 00:48:04.670
A body, nope, head-only robots,

00:48:04.670 --> 00:48:06.220
they were perfectly happy with.

00:48:07.056 --> 00:48:08.830
(muttering)

00:48:08.830 --> 00:48:09.748
What's that?

00:48:09.748 --> 00:48:10.581
(muttering)

00:48:10.581 --> 00:48:11.860
Eyebrows, no, it has these weird little,

00:48:11.860 --> 00:48:14.193
like, brownish eyebrows up there, yeah.

00:48:14.193 --> 00:48:16.300
They're kind of floating
up in midair, too.

00:48:16.300 --> 00:48:18.622
They were perfectly happy with that.

00:48:18.622 --> 00:48:20.170
(muttering)

00:48:20.170 --> 00:48:21.710
Nope.

00:48:21.710 --> 00:48:23.530
I'm gonna give you the answer,

00:48:23.530 --> 00:48:25.563
but the more interesting thing is why.

00:48:26.810 --> 00:48:31.143
The thing that this robot is
missing is a lower eyelid.

00:48:34.170 --> 00:48:37.833
It has an upper eyelid,
but no lower eyelid.

00:48:39.280 --> 00:48:41.720
Now, why is that important?

00:48:41.720 --> 00:48:42.750
None of us would have guessed.

00:48:42.750 --> 00:48:43.900
I would have guessed nose.

00:48:43.900 --> 00:48:45.843
That would have been my first guess.

00:48:47.150 --> 00:48:50.020
Why is that lower eyelid
the thing that was making

00:48:50.020 --> 00:48:54.283
them think this robot
was so angry and scary?

00:48:56.810 --> 00:48:58.990
Now, I grew up in New Jersey.

00:48:58.990 --> 00:49:01.630
To me, when this robot smile like this,

00:49:01.630 --> 00:49:03.900
and babbled at me like an infant,

00:49:03.900 --> 00:49:06.400
I thought it was friendly;
I thought it was happy.

00:49:08.930 --> 00:49:13.350
But culturally, these girls,
who were raised in Japan,

00:49:13.350 --> 00:49:17.930
in a culture where facial
expression is important,

00:49:17.930 --> 00:49:21.223
but not as prevalent as things
like postural expression,

00:49:22.490 --> 00:49:25.563
they saw this robot that
didn't have a body at all,

00:49:26.530 --> 00:49:28.430
but they thought this robot was angry,

00:49:29.570 --> 00:49:33.203
because you could always see
the white underneath the pupil.

00:49:35.210 --> 00:49:37.500
And the only time you do that with humans

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:40.387
is when they're leaning
forward and looking up,

00:49:40.387 --> 00:49:42.480
and their eyes are rolled up,

00:49:42.480 --> 00:49:45.423
and you can see that
white all under the pupil.

00:49:46.410 --> 00:49:48.730
And so even though this
robot doesn't have a body,

00:49:48.730 --> 00:49:52.753
they felt like this robot
was aggressive and angry.

00:49:53.890 --> 00:49:55.790
And they wanted nothing to do with it.

00:49:56.980 --> 00:49:59.063
Because it was missing the lower eyelid.

00:50:01.250 --> 00:50:03.480
One of the things that robots do

00:50:03.480 --> 00:50:05.790
is they make us pay attention to details

00:50:05.790 --> 00:50:08.093
that we would otherwise miss completely.

00:50:09.170 --> 00:50:12.330
We've looked, and in all
the history of animation

00:50:12.330 --> 00:50:15.220
that I can find, in all
of the psychology about

00:50:15.220 --> 00:50:19.920
facial expression, nowhere
can I find a rule that says:

00:50:19.920 --> 00:50:22.130
lower eyelids are
important for this reason,

00:50:22.130 --> 00:50:23.583
in these cultures.

00:50:25.700 --> 00:50:28.603
But we discovered that with these systems.

00:50:29.710 --> 00:50:32.713
Sometimes we uncover these little things,

00:50:33.730 --> 00:50:36.083
where the details really do matter.

00:50:37.560 --> 00:50:39.740
And for these children, it was dramatic.

00:50:39.740 --> 00:50:42.530
It was the difference between
this is a cute, fun partner,

00:50:42.530 --> 00:50:44.293
and this thing is out to get me.

00:50:48.200 --> 00:50:50.250
So robots are a little
bit unique in that they do

00:50:50.250 --> 00:50:53.113
force us to pay attention
to those details.

00:50:55.590 --> 00:51:00.190
But more than that, sometimes
we can use these tools

00:51:01.280 --> 00:51:04.373
to help define that frontier even more.

00:51:05.330 --> 00:51:08.540
I started out by drawing this line between

00:51:08.540 --> 00:51:10.340
the things that we treated like objects

00:51:10.340 --> 00:51:13.403
and the things that we
treated like agents.

00:51:14.340 --> 00:51:16.900
And in fact, for the
psychologists among you,

00:51:16.900 --> 00:51:18.363
that's no news.

00:51:19.660 --> 00:51:21.490
You would probably
point me, at this point,

00:51:21.490 --> 00:51:26.490
back to the 1944 studies of
Heider and Simmel, and say:

00:51:26.530 --> 00:51:29.570
look, we've known for many, many years

00:51:29.570 --> 00:51:33.390
that you can take things
that are just shapes

00:51:33.390 --> 00:51:37.220
on a screen, and make those shapes move

00:51:37.220 --> 00:51:40.650
and tell a story with them,
and that when people watch

00:51:40.650 --> 00:51:44.080
that story, and describe it later,

00:51:44.080 --> 00:51:46.750
they won't describe it
in terms of positions

00:51:46.750 --> 00:51:50.190
and orientations of those shapes.

00:51:50.190 --> 00:51:55.190
They'll define it in terms of
relationships and feelings.

00:51:55.620 --> 00:51:58.630
They'll tell you who's
frustrated at the end.

00:51:58.630 --> 00:52:01.270
They'll tell you which
two shapes were friends,

00:52:01.270 --> 00:52:02.623
and who was a bully.

00:52:04.320 --> 00:52:07.223
When in fact, they were
shapes on a screen.

00:52:08.310 --> 00:52:09.380
They didn't have thoughts

00:52:09.380 --> 00:52:12.850
or feelings or desires or friendships.

00:52:12.850 --> 00:52:14.173
But we do that naturally.

00:52:15.310 --> 00:52:17.890
In fact, we train people to do that.

00:52:17.890 --> 00:52:18.973
We call it animation.

00:52:20.020 --> 00:52:23.453
We train people to give
life to these creatures.

00:52:25.090 --> 00:52:26.597
And in fact, most of what we know

00:52:26.597 --> 00:52:29.010
and the way that this happens,

00:52:29.010 --> 00:52:32.260
even within robotics, is
that we follow these rules

00:52:32.260 --> 00:52:35.070
that have been outlined
in art and animation,

00:52:35.070 --> 00:52:37.043
and in psychology and psychophysics,

00:52:38.490 --> 00:52:41.063
that are all based around
the way things move.

00:52:43.336 --> 00:52:45.070
And one of the wonderful
things you find out with robots

00:52:45.070 --> 00:52:47.873
is that sometimes there
are other factors at play.

00:52:49.390 --> 00:52:53.330
So let me show you, again,
another study designed

00:52:53.330 --> 00:52:56.040
by Yale undergraduates that, again,

00:52:56.040 --> 00:52:59.860
has been replicated by at
least 50 groups worldwide,

00:52:59.860 --> 00:53:02.720
that even 10 years after
its first appearance

00:53:02.720 --> 00:53:07.130
is still being refined, and
this was a really simple,

00:53:07.130 --> 00:53:10.313
little study that involved
a robot that cheated.

00:53:11.540 --> 00:53:14.440
So here was this two and a
half foot tall robot before,

00:53:14.440 --> 00:53:16.620
that you saw telling
people to throw away books

00:53:16.620 --> 00:53:17.673
in the trash can.

00:53:18.810 --> 00:53:21.000
And this time, we brought
people in and we said:

00:53:21.000 --> 00:53:22.360
you're gonna sit and you're gonna play

00:53:22.360 --> 00:53:24.363
rock, paper, scissors with this robot.

00:53:25.740 --> 00:53:28.870
And so they sat and they played

00:53:28.870 --> 00:53:32.440
20 minutes of rock, paper, scissors.

00:53:32.440 --> 00:53:34.300
20 minutes of rock, paper, scissors,

00:53:34.300 --> 00:53:37.200
in case you do not have a toddler,

00:53:37.200 --> 00:53:40.563
is about the most mind-numbing
thing that you can imagine.

00:53:42.020 --> 00:53:45.580
20 minutes and we had undergraduates
clawing their eyes out,

00:53:45.580 --> 00:53:47.123
ready to get out of the room.

00:53:48.860 --> 00:53:50.403
- [Robot] Yes, I win.

00:53:51.460 --> 00:53:52.983
We have tied this round.

00:53:53.886 --> 00:53:54.803
Yes, I win.

00:53:56.364 --> 00:53:57.364
Aw, you win.

00:53:58.570 --> 00:54:00.113
We have tied this round.

00:54:01.110 --> 00:54:04.290
- And even though this robot was moving,

00:54:04.290 --> 00:54:07.890
and seemingly making decisions
about what gesture to throw,

00:54:07.890 --> 00:54:11.840
and talking to them and
telling them, I win, you win,

00:54:11.840 --> 00:54:16.160
we tied, and seemingly
seeing what they were doing,

00:54:16.160 --> 00:54:16.993
and responding,

00:54:19.840 --> 00:54:24.410
by 20 minutes in, this
thing was not an agent.

00:54:24.410 --> 00:54:25.563
It was an object.

00:54:27.760 --> 00:54:30.380
In the words of one of our undergraduates,

00:54:30.380 --> 00:54:32.193
it was a boring toaster.

00:54:34.320 --> 00:54:37.670
Now, for half of those
who came in and did this,

00:54:37.670 --> 00:54:42.670
after 20 minutes, we had
one unusual thing happen.

00:54:44.430 --> 00:54:46.660
Which is the robot would throw a gesture,

00:54:46.660 --> 00:54:48.800
the person would throw a gesture,

00:54:48.800 --> 00:54:53.800
the robot would lose, but it
would incorrectly say: I win.

00:54:56.500 --> 00:55:00.113
And when that happened,
people were surprised.

00:55:04.675 --> 00:55:06.468
- I'm not so sure about that.

00:55:06.468 --> 00:55:07.301
- What?

00:55:08.308 --> 00:55:09.141
- We tied.

00:55:10.142 --> 00:55:10.975
- How?

00:55:12.922 --> 00:55:14.350
- I don't think so.

00:55:14.350 --> 00:55:16.790
- They thought it was unusual,
they thought something

00:55:16.790 --> 00:55:19.603
interesting had happened,
they often said something,

00:55:21.030 --> 00:55:24.410
but then the robot went back
to its normal operation.

00:55:24.410 --> 00:55:26.070
And they figured it was a glitch,

00:55:26.070 --> 00:55:30.243
it was a malfunction, it
was still just an object.

00:55:32.490 --> 00:55:35.823
We made them play 20 more
minutes of rock, paper, scissors.

00:55:37.260 --> 00:55:40.343
We paid them not nearly
enough money for this.

00:55:41.450 --> 00:55:43.470
And by the end, this group came out,

00:55:43.470 --> 00:55:46.010
after 40 minutes of rock, paper, scissors,

00:55:46.010 --> 00:55:49.410
and they said: hand me
the cash, I'm out of here.

00:55:49.410 --> 00:55:51.000
We said: did anything unusual happen?

00:55:51.000 --> 00:55:53.023
They said: cash first.

00:55:53.940 --> 00:55:54.773
They were done.

00:55:56.690 --> 00:56:00.660
The other half played 20 boring minutes,

00:56:00.660 --> 00:56:02.970
and then one unusual thing happened.

00:56:02.970 --> 00:56:05.420
The robot threw a gesture
and they threw a gesture,

00:56:05.420 --> 00:56:07.033
and even though they had won,

00:56:08.190 --> 00:56:12.063
the robot slowly changed
its hand position.

00:56:13.310 --> 00:56:15.796
And then said: I win.

00:56:15.796 --> 00:56:18.046
(laughing)

00:56:19.000 --> 00:56:22.287
And now their response
was completely different.

00:56:24.900 --> 00:56:26.752
- [Robot] Yes, I win.

00:56:26.752 --> 00:56:29.002
(laughing)

00:56:31.996 --> 00:56:34.079
- Ah, you sneaky bastard.

00:56:35.812 --> 00:56:37.600
- [Robot] Yes, I win.

00:56:37.600 --> 00:56:38.573
- That's cheating!

00:56:40.940 --> 00:56:42.143
- [Robot] Yes, I win.

00:56:44.134 --> 00:56:45.467
- You're tricky.

00:56:47.809 --> 00:56:49.479
- [Robot] Yes, I win.

00:56:49.479 --> 00:56:52.200
(laughing)

00:56:52.200 --> 00:56:53.810
- And they thought that this was funny,

00:56:53.810 --> 00:56:54.643
they thought this was interesting,

00:56:54.643 --> 00:56:57.410
some of them got upset about this.

00:56:57.410 --> 00:57:00.760
But then in the second 20
minutes they sat there,

00:57:00.760 --> 00:57:03.310
they kept talking to the robot.

00:57:03.310 --> 00:57:04.300
None of them had talked to the robot

00:57:04.300 --> 00:57:06.460
in the first 20 minutes.

00:57:06.460 --> 00:57:08.367
One of them kept on this
20 minute dialog of:

00:57:08.367 --> 00:57:11.171
now you better not cheat this time!

00:57:11.171 --> 00:57:14.020
I'm watching you; I'm still watching you!

00:57:14.020 --> 00:57:16.593
I remember, I'm not
letting you cheat again!

00:57:19.060 --> 00:57:22.580
20 minutes later, they walked back in.

00:57:22.580 --> 00:57:25.300
We walked them out of the room,

00:57:25.300 --> 00:57:26.550
and they didn't care about the payment.

00:57:26.550 --> 00:57:29.198
They said: did you know that robot cheats?

00:57:29.198 --> 00:57:31.710
(laughing)

00:57:31.710 --> 00:57:33.960
They wanted to make sure
that somebody in charge

00:57:33.960 --> 00:57:37.800
knew that this was happening,
because that's not supposed

00:57:37.800 --> 00:57:39.383
to be the way it goes.

00:57:40.540 --> 00:57:43.730
20 minutes later, they
were still believing

00:57:43.730 --> 00:57:45.830
that this thing was an agent.

00:57:45.830 --> 00:57:47.010
It was still out to get them.

00:57:47.010 --> 00:57:49.720
They made eye contact with
it, they thought it was more

00:57:49.720 --> 00:57:51.850
intelligent than the
others, they thought it was

00:57:51.850 --> 00:57:54.490
more interesting to play with
and had better strategies.

00:57:54.490 --> 00:57:59.470
It was better looking
than the other robots.

00:57:59.470 --> 00:58:01.323
I still don't understand that one.

00:58:02.270 --> 00:58:04.773
But everything for them changed.

00:58:06.450 --> 00:58:09.893
And what was really hard
is that it didn't stop.

00:58:10.740 --> 00:58:13.500
All of our other motion-based
techniques for generating

00:58:13.500 --> 00:58:15.870
agency and animacy,
they're the kinds of things

00:58:15.870 --> 00:58:18.253
we can turn on and turn off so easily.

00:58:19.270 --> 00:58:20.433
This one wasn't.

00:58:21.700 --> 00:58:23.953
It was very hard for
us to get them to stop.

00:58:25.500 --> 00:58:28.900
What's even more interesting
is that this is very specific

00:58:28.900 --> 00:58:31.660
to it stealing your victory.

00:58:31.660 --> 00:58:35.350
If in fact the robot cheats,
but cheats in your favor,

00:58:35.350 --> 00:58:38.143
if it gives you a victory
you don't deserve,

00:58:39.310 --> 00:58:41.800
you could care less.

00:58:41.800 --> 00:58:44.450
It's not an agent; that
was just a malfunction.

00:58:44.450 --> 00:58:45.793
You're just lucky.

00:58:47.500 --> 00:58:50.620
In fact, here's a different
robot doing that same thing.

00:58:50.620 --> 00:58:52.460
When the robot cheats in order to lose,

00:58:52.460 --> 00:58:54.040
throws you a victory.

00:58:54.040 --> 00:58:55.090
- [Robot] Let's play.

00:58:56.170 --> 00:58:59.103
- We get confusion, we get uncertainty.

00:59:00.787 --> 00:59:02.234
- [Robot] Oh, you win.

00:59:02.234 --> 00:59:03.151
Let's play.

00:59:04.945 --> 00:59:06.144
Oh, you win.

00:59:06.144 --> 00:59:07.061
Let's play.

00:59:09.922 --> 00:59:11.850
- But when that robot
steals your victory...

00:59:11.850 --> 00:59:12.860
- [Robot] Yes, I win.

00:59:12.860 --> 00:59:15.557
Let's play.
- No, you can't do that.

00:59:16.460 --> 00:59:19.060
- You are not ready to put up with that.

00:59:19.060 --> 00:59:19.893
- What?!

00:59:19.893 --> 00:59:20.807
- I win.
- You cheated!

00:59:24.574 --> 00:59:25.907
You cheated now.

00:59:26.805 --> 00:59:29.651
- I win.
- Hey, you big cheater!

00:59:29.651 --> 00:59:32.041
You cheat, super cheat!

00:59:32.041 --> 00:59:34.291
(laughing)

00:59:35.810 --> 00:59:37.910
- I have so many hours
of Yale undergraduates

00:59:37.910 --> 00:59:40.630
swearing at robots, you
would not believe it.

00:59:40.630 --> 00:59:45.630
So, ah, so let me say
this just at the end.

00:59:46.060 --> 00:59:48.020
One of the things that's
really unique about these

00:59:48.020 --> 00:59:51.930
is robots let us investigate things

00:59:51.930 --> 00:59:55.130
about that frontier, about that boundary,

00:59:55.130 --> 00:59:58.980
that we couldn't investigate with people.

00:59:58.980 --> 01:00:01.000
It's hard for me to
produce for you a person

01:00:01.000 --> 01:00:02.643
with no lower eyelid.

01:00:03.840 --> 01:00:06.580
It's hard for me to produce
a person that you're willing

01:00:06.580 --> 01:00:08.510
to sit and play this game against,

01:00:08.510 --> 01:00:11.783
where you're uncertain what
their agency status is.

01:00:13.530 --> 01:00:15.230
But we can do that with the robot.

01:00:16.140 --> 01:00:18.720
And we can uncover things.

01:00:18.720 --> 01:00:21.920
Now, as a very last thing,
I can ask the question:

01:00:21.920 --> 01:00:23.550
why is it important?

01:00:23.550 --> 01:00:26.000
Why do I need to know what
that boundary is like?

01:00:26.000 --> 01:00:27.653
Why should I care about that?

01:00:29.640 --> 01:00:33.850
Well, one of the real interesting things

01:00:33.850 --> 01:00:37.313
is that no matter what we end up doing,

01:00:38.670 --> 01:00:41.440
those unique parts of the boundary

01:00:41.440 --> 01:00:43.610
are often where we find
the most interesting

01:00:43.610 --> 01:00:46.463
and exciting applications
for these robots.

01:00:47.580 --> 01:00:50.060
I told you before that
you are more compliant

01:00:50.060 --> 01:00:52.010
with a physical robot
than other technology,

01:00:52.010 --> 01:00:54.733
you'll learn faster with a physical robot.

01:00:55.890 --> 01:00:58.740
That's one of the reasons we
use robots for tutoring now.

01:00:59.680 --> 01:01:02.280
In fact, last year, we
started doing in-home tutoring

01:01:02.280 --> 01:01:06.430
for kids with autism, with
a little robot platform.

01:01:06.430 --> 01:01:09.460
And the kids would sit and
play social skills games

01:01:09.460 --> 01:01:11.053
with these robots.

01:01:11.910 --> 01:01:13.790
They would play the same kinds of games

01:01:13.790 --> 01:01:16.230
that they would in therapy,

01:01:16.230 --> 01:01:18.670
and they did them in the really complex

01:01:18.670 --> 01:01:20.683
and difficult environment of the home.

01:01:22.670 --> 01:01:25.380
And I don't have a ton
of time to talk about it,

01:01:25.380 --> 01:01:27.753
so I'm gonna skip past and just say,

01:01:28.990 --> 01:01:31.930
let's let one of the
parents who've done this,

01:01:31.930 --> 01:01:33.773
actually say something about it.

01:01:34.740 --> 01:01:37.130
- He is so excited to
spend his half an hour

01:01:37.130 --> 01:01:39.450
helping the robot find his way home,

01:01:39.450 --> 01:01:42.240
and he will come on, and
I will set it up for him,

01:01:42.240 --> 01:01:46.030
turn on the robot, and he
is engaged immediately.

01:01:46.030 --> 01:01:47.920
Having the ability to have this robot

01:01:47.920 --> 01:01:50.010
and to do an hour of therapy with him,

01:01:50.010 --> 01:01:52.870
that was really specific to
what he needs to focus on

01:01:52.870 --> 01:01:56.660
was just, it seemed like a
great opportunity for him.

01:01:56.660 --> 01:01:59.630
It's really been just, he's
been engaged and intrigued,

01:01:59.630 --> 01:02:03.160
and sometimes therapies are,
they get a little exhausting

01:02:03.160 --> 01:02:05.250
and he's not excited, and
this one wasn't like that.

01:02:05.250 --> 01:02:09.570
He was ready to play
at 7:30 in the morning.

01:02:09.570 --> 01:02:12.830
It's that, we went to
the playground one day,

01:02:12.830 --> 01:02:15.640
and I noticed immediately
there was a group of children

01:02:15.640 --> 01:02:19.480
playing in the sand, and he
walked up to them and said:

01:02:19.480 --> 01:02:21.430
hi, what are you doing?

01:02:21.430 --> 01:02:24.397
And I don't think he would
have done that a month ago.

01:02:27.150 --> 01:02:30.490
- This family in particular,
even though with all

01:02:30.490 --> 01:02:35.110
of our families we saw kids
make clinically verified skill

01:02:35.110 --> 01:02:37.840
improvements, the first
time a piece of technology

01:02:37.840 --> 01:02:42.840
has done that, where we saw
kids not only try to avoid

01:02:43.178 --> 01:02:47.250
their therapy or skate by,
but actively engaging with it.

01:02:47.250 --> 01:02:49.480
We saw the parents being thrilled with it,

01:02:49.480 --> 01:02:52.753
we saw the skills start to
bleed over to the playground.

01:02:54.150 --> 01:02:58.180
This family, they found out
midway through that month

01:02:58.180 --> 01:03:01.440
that they had that robot,
the husband got a new job,

01:03:01.440 --> 01:03:02.820
got transferred to the west coast,

01:03:02.820 --> 01:03:04.720
they were supposed to move right away.

01:03:05.900 --> 01:03:08.920
They shipped all of their
stuff out of their house.

01:03:08.920 --> 01:03:11.503
That's why all of those shelves are bare.

01:03:12.430 --> 01:03:16.200
And they decided for the last three weeks,

01:03:16.200 --> 01:03:19.310
they were gonna sleep in
sleeping bags on the floor

01:03:19.310 --> 01:03:21.630
so they could stay with the robot,

01:03:21.630 --> 01:03:24.283
because of how much that
was meaning to their son.

01:03:26.080 --> 01:03:29.290
That's what we get when
we start to characterize

01:03:29.290 --> 01:03:30.680
this boundary.

01:03:30.680 --> 01:03:34.033
We get systems where we really
can make an impact on people.

01:03:35.390 --> 01:03:37.610
All right, let me end
just by saying thanks

01:03:37.610 --> 01:03:38.910
to a whole lot of people.

01:03:38.910 --> 01:03:41.920
If you want any other
information, our website's there,

01:03:41.920 --> 01:03:45.040
and I'm happy to take questions
until you're tired of us.

01:03:45.040 --> 01:03:46.922
So thank you.

01:03:46.922 --> 01:03:49.339
(applauding)

01:03:54.929 --> 01:03:55.801
Go ahead.

01:03:55.801 --> 01:03:56.660
(unintelligible muttering)

01:03:56.660 --> 01:03:58.158
Yeah.

01:03:58.158 --> 01:03:58.991
(muttering)

01:03:58.991 --> 01:04:00.103
Oh wait, they want you on.

01:04:01.700 --> 01:04:04.160
- [Attendee] So my observation
is I'm a lot less patient

01:04:04.160 --> 01:04:07.660
with my virtual assistants
than I am with real people.

01:04:07.660 --> 01:04:12.660
- Yes, ah, and it's
interesting because one

01:04:13.540 --> 01:04:16.800
of the things that we see
with real, physical robots,

01:04:16.800 --> 01:04:20.080
is that people are much
more willing to adjust

01:04:20.080 --> 01:04:22.810
when the robots make mistakes.

01:04:22.810 --> 01:04:24.600
And I don't know if that's
an embodiment effect

01:04:24.600 --> 01:04:27.370
that carries over or not,
but we see it all the time.

01:04:27.370 --> 01:04:31.540
We build systems that do
manufacturing with people.

01:04:31.540 --> 01:04:35.130
So side by side, you're building
a birdhouse with the robot.

01:04:35.130 --> 01:04:36.930
And in fact, when the
robot makes a mistake

01:04:36.930 --> 01:04:39.320
and drops a tool, they
don't get mad at the robot.

01:04:39.320 --> 01:04:40.170
They help it out.

01:04:41.150 --> 01:04:43.100
We see that happen over and over again.

01:04:51.240 --> 01:04:52.940
- [Attendee] Thanks very
much for an interesting talk.

01:04:52.940 --> 01:04:57.350
So on the very last work
with the autistic child,

01:04:57.350 --> 01:04:59.360
it's unethical but I'll ask anyway,

01:04:59.360 --> 01:05:02.180
was there a controlled
experiment where the robot

01:05:02.180 --> 01:05:04.300
was on a screen instead of in person?

01:05:04.300 --> 01:05:06.740
- Yeah, we don't do a control
there because we know,

01:05:06.740 --> 01:05:08.490
in fact, that these kids will not learn

01:05:08.490 --> 01:05:10.210
from things on the screen.

01:05:10.210 --> 01:05:12.500
We've had a couple of other
studies that predate that,

01:05:12.500 --> 01:05:13.510
that show it.

01:05:13.510 --> 01:05:16.533
What we do use as a control
though, is we track the children

01:05:16.533 --> 01:05:19.400
the month before they get the robot,

01:05:19.400 --> 01:05:20.890
then a month with the robot,

01:05:20.890 --> 01:05:23.910
and then a month after
the robot has gone away.

01:05:23.910 --> 01:05:27.040
And in fact, those kids,
I'm sure I can just

01:05:27.040 --> 01:05:29.240
show you the data,
that's the easiest thing.

01:05:30.810 --> 01:05:34.330
Those kids in the month
before the robot shows up,

01:05:34.330 --> 01:05:36.560
they show no difference
in the social skills

01:05:36.560 --> 01:05:37.730
that we measure.

01:05:37.730 --> 01:05:41.070
A month with the robot and
they start showing improvement.

01:05:41.070 --> 01:05:42.457
But you take the robot away,

01:05:42.457 --> 01:05:45.450
and that improvement starts to fade away.

01:05:45.450 --> 01:05:47.570
Now, that's not, in fact, unusual,

01:05:47.570 --> 01:05:50.170
and that's in fact what we expect to see.

01:05:50.170 --> 01:05:52.750
Because a month with a personal therapist,

01:05:52.750 --> 01:05:55.600
sitting in your home,
100 percent of the time,

01:05:55.600 --> 01:05:58.720
you don't get lasting
effects after just a month.

01:05:58.720 --> 01:06:00.310
So if we had gotten that with the robot,

01:06:00.310 --> 01:06:02.480
something would have been wrong.

01:06:02.480 --> 01:06:06.950
So, we know that that's
actually sort of exactly

01:06:06.950 --> 01:06:10.453
the pattern that we wanted to see, sure.

01:06:11.490 --> 01:06:13.570
- [Attendee] In the
case of the robot making

01:06:13.570 --> 01:06:17.150
facial expressions that frightened
away some of the students

01:06:17.150 --> 01:06:19.940
did those students recognize immediately

01:06:19.940 --> 01:06:21.820
that the reason they were frightened was

01:06:21.820 --> 01:06:23.780
because it was missing a lower eyelid?

01:06:23.780 --> 01:06:25.450
- No, so they couldn't tell us that.

01:06:25.450 --> 01:06:28.172
And that was really what was,
in part, really interesting.

01:06:28.172 --> 01:06:29.260
The could tell us what they thought.

01:06:29.260 --> 01:06:30.710
They thought the robot was aggressive,

01:06:30.710 --> 01:06:33.463
they thought the robot was angry, okay?

01:06:34.330 --> 01:06:38.440
But they couldn't pinpoint
exactly why that was.

01:06:38.440 --> 01:06:40.500
That took a little bit of
experiment on our part,

01:06:40.500 --> 01:06:41.660
in order to figure that out,

01:06:41.660 --> 01:06:43.090
and it actually took
us a couple of months.

01:06:43.090 --> 01:06:46.109
So don't feel bad that
you missed the eyelid.

01:06:46.109 --> 01:06:47.610
You know, we put that thing together,

01:06:47.610 --> 01:06:49.860
and it still took us
months to figure it out.

01:06:51.160 --> 01:06:51.993
Yeah.

01:06:51.993 --> 01:06:53.750
- [Attendee] Thank you so
much for this wonderful talk.

01:06:53.750 --> 01:06:57.730
I wonder if there are
studies about interactions

01:06:57.730 --> 01:07:00.040
between children and toys and whether toys

01:07:00.040 --> 01:07:05.040
are kind of an intermediate
agent between humans and robots?

01:07:05.210 --> 01:07:08.470
- Yeah, and you have to now
talk about different ages

01:07:08.470 --> 01:07:10.710
of children, as well, and in fact,

01:07:10.710 --> 01:07:12.730
one of the really interesting
things that you see

01:07:12.730 --> 01:07:15.630
is you see young kids
who are willing to take,

01:07:15.630 --> 01:07:19.850
let's say, a stuffed doll
and to have a pretend

01:07:19.850 --> 01:07:22.320
relationship with that doll, or for them,

01:07:22.320 --> 01:07:23.950
what is probably a real relationship,

01:07:23.950 --> 01:07:27.800
but with this character that
does not reciprocate in any way

01:07:28.710 --> 01:07:30.620
and you introduce a thing with technology

01:07:30.620 --> 01:07:33.660
that does have that kind of interactivity,

01:07:33.660 --> 01:07:37.140
and that relationship
becomes even stronger.

01:07:37.140 --> 01:07:39.890
We've seen these kinds of things
with robots being developed

01:07:39.890 --> 01:07:41.230
for kindergarten.

01:07:41.230 --> 01:07:44.580
We've even done experiments
with, ah, robots with kids

01:07:44.580 --> 01:07:47.730
as young as six-month old, deaf infants,

01:07:47.730 --> 01:07:50.730
learning sign language from robots.

01:07:50.730 --> 01:07:53.300
So we've seen this very, very early on.

01:07:53.300 --> 01:07:56.410
There are similarities to the
way that they engage with toys

01:07:56.410 --> 01:07:58.480
but also some things that are different,

01:07:58.480 --> 01:08:00.423
based on the age appropriateness.

01:08:01.810 --> 01:08:03.230
It's a really good
question that we don't know

01:08:03.230 --> 01:08:04.580
enough about, though, yeah.

01:08:09.190 --> 01:08:11.468
- [Attendee] Brian, thank
you, that was brilliant.

01:08:11.468 --> 01:08:12.801
- Oh, thank you.

01:08:14.480 --> 01:08:16.140
- [Attendee] I'm thinking
of the difference

01:08:16.140 --> 01:08:18.677
between a robot and human,

01:08:18.677 --> 01:08:22.133
and robot and human-controlled robot.

01:08:24.420 --> 01:08:28.423
In the context of the
books and the trash can.

01:08:30.980 --> 01:08:35.820
Would you say, what do
you think about that?

01:08:35.820 --> 01:08:39.440
If the robot were to be human-controlled,

01:08:39.440 --> 01:08:44.020
or if they had a human
telling them: throw it away.

01:08:44.020 --> 01:08:46.620
- Yeah, so in many of these cases,

01:08:46.620 --> 01:08:49.970
whether you perceive that
the robot is autonomous

01:08:49.970 --> 01:08:52.430
does in fact make a difference.

01:08:52.430 --> 01:08:55.560
So if I know, if I'm told there's a person

01:08:55.560 --> 01:08:57.930
who's controlling this
system in the other room--

01:08:57.930 --> 01:08:58.938
(muttering)

01:08:58.938 --> 01:08:59.946
No, no, go ahead.

01:08:59.946 --> 01:09:01.220
- [Attendee] If you're no
told that there's a human.

01:09:01.220 --> 01:09:05.160
- Right, so if I am told,
then there's a response,

01:09:05.160 --> 01:09:07.540
and I treat the robot like it's a person.

01:09:07.540 --> 01:09:10.560
Because I know that it's a
person making the decision.

01:09:10.560 --> 01:09:15.293
If I'm not told then I have to
make this uncertain judgment.

01:09:16.130 --> 01:09:19.830
Sometimes people do think that
there's a human in control,

01:09:19.830 --> 01:09:22.810
when in fact it's not,
and sometimes in fact,

01:09:22.810 --> 01:09:25.200
people will do the exact opposite.

01:09:25.200 --> 01:09:27.240
That is, they'll think it's
autonomous when in fact,

01:09:27.240 --> 01:09:28.683
there is a human in control.

01:09:29.920 --> 01:09:33.050
One of the common methods
that we use in this field

01:09:33.050 --> 01:09:37.380
is a prototype study called
a Wizard of Oz study.

01:09:37.380 --> 01:09:40.390
In which the robot is
being secretly controlled

01:09:40.390 --> 01:09:44.020
by a human in the other room,
but you're not told that,

01:09:44.020 --> 01:09:47.040
and we in fact provoke the
illusion that there is,

01:09:47.040 --> 01:09:49.700
in fact, full autonomy with the robot,

01:09:49.700 --> 01:09:52.683
even though there is this secret
wizard behind the curtain.

01:09:53.780 --> 01:09:57.050
And we do that as a prototyping
device often because

01:09:57.050 --> 01:10:01.080
the cost of developing that
autonomy within the robot

01:10:01.080 --> 01:10:04.750
is substantial, and if we just want to see

01:10:04.750 --> 01:10:08.453
what the effect would be,
we'll pretend in this way.

01:10:09.770 --> 01:10:12.790
We ran one study, in fact,
where there was a robot

01:10:12.790 --> 01:10:16.420
on a table, and I was standing
next to the participant,

01:10:16.420 --> 01:10:17.843
and I had a clipboard.

01:10:18.860 --> 01:10:22.000
And in fact, I was controlling
the robot the entire time,

01:10:22.000 --> 01:10:24.690
because taped to the
bottom of the clipboard

01:10:24.690 --> 01:10:28.040
was a television remote,
where I could push

01:10:28.040 --> 01:10:31.193
different buttons on the remote
and get the robot to behave.

01:10:32.050 --> 01:10:36.090
We ran that protocol with
over 300 Yale undergraduates.

01:10:36.090 --> 01:10:38.820
Not one of them ever picked up on the fact

01:10:38.820 --> 01:10:41.393
that I was running the
robot right next to them.

01:10:43.370 --> 01:10:45.560
When we see these systems
and when we're designing

01:10:45.560 --> 01:10:49.190
them properly, you want to believe.

01:10:49.190 --> 01:10:50.640
It's not even that we do a good job.

01:10:50.640 --> 01:10:52.460
I'd like to think we do a good job,

01:10:52.460 --> 01:10:55.473
but you want to believe
that it's autonomous.

01:10:56.410 --> 01:10:59.613
So in fact, that's
almost our default case.

01:11:04.300 --> 01:11:07.110
- [Attendee] I was wondering,
in the conformity experiment

01:11:07.110 --> 01:11:11.320
with Dixit, if you had one
group that had a person

01:11:11.320 --> 01:11:14.660
giving the clue, and then
robots following afterwards

01:11:14.660 --> 01:11:18.600
with a similar but different
answer than the other human?

01:11:18.600 --> 01:11:20.650
- Yeah, so we didn't do that.

01:11:20.650 --> 01:11:22.980
We did a pilot version of
that with the Dixit study.

01:11:22.980 --> 01:11:25.830
It didn't matter if it was
a human giving the word

01:11:25.830 --> 01:11:28.840
or a robot giving the word,
it didn't seem to matter.

01:11:28.840 --> 01:11:31.750
There are some cases where
when we substitute a human

01:11:31.750 --> 01:11:35.200
in for the robot, it
does make a difference.

01:11:35.200 --> 01:11:37.470
If you remember that
study about the effect

01:11:37.470 --> 01:11:41.990
of embodiment on learning
games, with the nonograms,

01:11:41.990 --> 01:11:44.890
that sort of Sudoku-like puzzle,

01:11:44.890 --> 01:11:47.880
and I told you if you have
the robot sitting next to you,

01:11:47.880 --> 01:11:50.810
you improve by six minutes.

01:11:50.810 --> 01:11:52.930
Well, one of the things
we don't like to report

01:11:52.930 --> 01:11:56.490
is we actually ran a fifth
version of that protocol,

01:11:56.490 --> 01:11:59.050
where instead of getting
those clues from a robot

01:11:59.050 --> 01:12:01.870
sitting next to you, we had
a human sitting next to you,

01:12:01.870 --> 01:12:04.380
giving you those clues.

01:12:04.380 --> 01:12:07.120
Okay, so here's the time
when everybody gets to guess.

01:12:07.120 --> 01:12:10.330
If no clues you improved by two minutes,

01:12:10.330 --> 01:12:13.760
two clues, you get clues but
you get them from the video,

01:12:13.760 --> 01:12:16.060
or just audio, you
improve by four minutes,

01:12:16.060 --> 01:12:18.550
with the robot, you
improve by six minutes.

01:12:18.550 --> 01:12:21.050
How much do you improve
if you have a human there?

01:12:22.621 --> 01:12:23.790
(muttering)

01:12:23.790 --> 01:12:25.250
None?

01:12:25.250 --> 01:12:26.390
Eight minutes?

01:12:26.390 --> 01:12:27.250
- [Attendee] It's worse?

01:12:27.250 --> 01:12:29.740
- Four, six?

01:12:29.740 --> 01:12:33.084
Okay, the actual answer is minus five.

01:12:33.084 --> 01:12:34.430
(laughing)

01:12:34.430 --> 01:12:37.763
People, in fact, get worse,
the longer we leave them there.

01:12:38.700 --> 01:12:39.533
Okay?

01:12:39.533 --> 01:12:41.890
And in fact, this is easy to understand.

01:12:41.890 --> 01:12:43.340
That I'll give you this option.

01:12:43.340 --> 01:12:45.230
Would you like to take the exam in a room

01:12:45.230 --> 01:12:47.620
all by yourself, or would
you like to take the exam,

01:12:47.620 --> 01:12:50.470
sitting in my office while I
stare at you the whole time?

01:12:50.470 --> 01:12:52.350
(laughing)

01:12:52.350 --> 01:12:55.150
I don't know anybody who's
gonna take the staring, right?

01:12:55.150 --> 01:12:57.210
When we sat a person there,
and even though they were

01:12:57.210 --> 01:12:59.300
giving them these helpful clues,

01:12:59.300 --> 01:13:02.230
people got nervous.

01:13:02.230 --> 01:13:05.120
And in fact, their performance
basically kept degrading

01:13:05.120 --> 01:13:06.640
over time.

01:13:06.640 --> 01:13:09.660
So there are some cases
where we don't report

01:13:09.660 --> 01:13:12.620
the human version, in
fact, because it makes such

01:13:12.620 --> 01:13:15.110
a difference that it doesn't, you know,

01:13:15.110 --> 01:13:17.340
there is, it's not really comparable.

01:13:17.340 --> 01:13:19.560
But most of the time, we
do try a human variant,

01:13:19.560 --> 01:13:20.850
and when it doesn't make any difference,

01:13:20.850 --> 01:13:22.450
we just usually don't report it.

01:13:24.280 --> 01:13:27.200
- [Attendee] Hi, so starting
from this last answer

01:13:27.200 --> 01:13:31.810
you gave in this fifth try, it seems to me

01:13:31.810 --> 01:13:36.660
that this frontier, right,
that shifts very much,

01:13:36.660 --> 01:13:39.460
not only depending on
what's on the other side,

01:13:39.460 --> 01:13:42.450
but also on the humans on
the, let's say on our side.

01:13:42.450 --> 01:13:47.450
So if you have people that
have been growing up in Japan,

01:13:47.660 --> 01:13:50.470
something looks, but if you have, ah,

01:13:50.470 --> 01:13:52.400
and if you have Yale undergrads,

01:13:52.400 --> 01:13:56.520
they probably behave in a,
so, so what I don't understand

01:13:56.520 --> 01:14:00.233
is what you call a frontier,
it seems to me like a very,

01:14:01.689 --> 01:14:06.689
variable, depending not only
on what's on the computer,

01:14:07.120 --> 01:14:09.960
or the machine on the
other side, but also who's,

01:14:09.960 --> 01:14:12.380
you know, I'm from Italy
and I was thinking:

01:14:12.380 --> 01:14:16.520
how would Italians react
to a computer cheating?

01:14:16.520 --> 01:14:17.353
- Yeah.

01:14:17.353 --> 01:14:20.290
- [Attendee] Right, because
we have a different cultural

01:14:20.290 --> 01:14:22.460
understanding of what is cheating,

01:14:22.460 --> 01:14:27.460
so you know, how generalizable
are these findings?

01:14:28.168 --> 01:14:29.597
This is my question.

01:14:29.597 --> 01:14:31.730
- Yeah, so I can give
you two answers to this.

01:14:31.730 --> 01:14:35.670
One is, we do know that
there are cultural effects,

01:14:35.670 --> 01:14:38.550
and age-related effects especially,

01:14:38.550 --> 01:14:40.800
that impact this boundary.

01:14:40.800 --> 01:14:43.530
And to some extent, we're okay with that.

01:14:43.530 --> 01:14:46.500
The idea that I have
different cultural boundaries

01:14:46.500 --> 01:14:51.230
that determine my perceptions,
that's, I would expect that.

01:14:51.230 --> 01:14:54.900
The idea that my perceptions
change between when I'm three

01:14:54.900 --> 01:14:58.320
years old and seven
years old and an adult?

01:14:58.320 --> 01:15:00.440
I'm willing to accept that.

01:15:00.440 --> 01:15:02.540
That's a standard part
of the way we think about

01:15:02.540 --> 01:15:04.410
the way we perceive the world.

01:15:04.410 --> 01:15:07.220
And so the fact that this
technology has this shifting

01:15:07.220 --> 01:15:11.070
boundary that's also
culturally determined,

01:15:11.070 --> 01:15:12.480
that I think is something that I think

01:15:12.480 --> 01:15:15.163
everyone in this field
would fully acknowledge.

01:15:17.010 --> 01:15:19.810
In terms of how repeatable
or how universal

01:15:19.810 --> 01:15:22.040
are these things, that's one of the things

01:15:22.040 --> 01:15:25.160
that when we find them,
when we find those ones

01:15:25.160 --> 01:15:29.270
that are universal, that
hold up across a wide range

01:15:29.270 --> 01:15:32.120
of circumstances, across
a wide range of cultures,

01:15:32.120 --> 01:15:35.320
across a range of ages, that's when things

01:15:35.320 --> 01:15:37.063
get to be really interesting.

01:15:38.410 --> 01:15:41.423
Some of these studies do
that, and some of them don't.

01:15:42.400 --> 01:15:44.370
For example, those conformity studies,

01:15:44.370 --> 01:15:46.250
the really simple one, the Asch studies

01:15:46.250 --> 01:15:48.960
with the matching the line lengths?

01:15:48.960 --> 01:15:51.730
In fact, if you do that with
robots and five year olds,

01:15:51.730 --> 01:15:54.730
and Tony Belpaeme has done this in the UK,

01:15:54.730 --> 01:15:57.233
the five year olds go
along with the robots.

01:15:58.130 --> 01:15:59.840
In fact, about two thirds of the time,

01:15:59.840 --> 01:16:02.870
they will change their answer
to go along with the robots.

01:16:02.870 --> 01:16:04.693
Adults won't do it, but kids will.

01:16:05.670 --> 01:16:08.140
For adults, we have to make
it slightly more subtle,

01:16:08.140 --> 01:16:10.580
in order for them to buy into it.

01:16:10.580 --> 01:16:11.820
Are there cultural differences

01:16:11.820 --> 01:16:13.290
across that conformity effect?

01:16:13.290 --> 01:16:16.530
Yeah, in fact, the Asch studies
have seen large cultural

01:16:16.530 --> 01:16:21.073
variations in human to human
effects of peer influence.

01:16:21.970 --> 01:16:24.970
So that's where we would
expect to see variations

01:16:24.970 --> 01:16:28.543
in robot influence on them as well.

01:16:29.810 --> 01:16:32.923
There are other studies, though,
that seem to be universal.

01:16:33.950 --> 01:16:37.010
That compliance result,
about throwing away things

01:16:37.010 --> 01:16:39.580
in the trash can, we've had groups,

01:16:39.580 --> 01:16:44.020
I think I'm up to counting
somewhere around 35 groups

01:16:44.020 --> 01:16:49.020
all over the US, Asia,
Europe, South America.

01:16:51.170 --> 01:16:53.370
I don't think we have
any out of Africa yet,

01:16:54.380 --> 01:16:57.290
but here we see lots
of different cultures,

01:16:57.290 --> 01:16:59.090
all of them showing the same result.

01:16:59.930 --> 01:17:01.780
That's when we start seeing
something and saying:

01:17:01.780 --> 01:17:03.580
maybe this is something fundamental.

01:17:05.610 --> 01:17:08.140
And so in fact, mapping
these not only in terms

01:17:08.140 --> 01:17:10.870
of just is there, where is the boundary?

01:17:10.870 --> 01:17:13.487
But when does the boundary shift?

01:17:13.487 --> 01:17:17.600
Does it vary based on age,
does it vary based on culture?

01:17:17.600 --> 01:17:20.043
That's part of the interesting data to us.

01:17:22.610 --> 01:17:25.043
I hope that answered your question.

01:17:25.043 --> 01:17:28.380
- [Attendee] A question about
the anthropomorphism aspect

01:17:28.380 --> 01:17:31.360
of the whole thing: we know,
for example, from the way

01:17:31.360 --> 01:17:34.170
people treat different
kinds of animals as pets,

01:17:34.170 --> 01:17:36.570
there are the fuzzy ones
and then there's frogs

01:17:36.570 --> 01:17:38.840
and so on that we care less about.

01:17:38.840 --> 01:17:41.550
Are there controls that
sort of take, I mean,

01:17:41.550 --> 01:17:46.550
you said one of your robots
had four eyes, or four cameras,

01:17:46.716 --> 01:17:49.030
I've already agreed that
they're eyes, right?

01:17:49.030 --> 01:17:49.910
- Right.

01:17:49.910 --> 01:17:53.240
- [Attendee] But the
empathy we have for things

01:17:53.240 --> 01:17:56.300
that look like us versus other, I mean,

01:17:56.300 --> 01:17:57.733
do you have a control with a robot

01:17:57.733 --> 01:18:00.460
that looks like a mollusc or something?

01:18:00.460 --> 01:18:04.540
- Yeah, so, there are
some things where, ah,

01:18:04.540 --> 01:18:06.620
you know, many of these
things, you have to realize,

01:18:06.620 --> 01:18:09.133
this is a field that's about 10 years old.

01:18:09.980 --> 01:18:11.430
And the number of things
that have been done

01:18:11.430 --> 01:18:13.393
exhaustively is very small.

01:18:14.420 --> 01:18:17.550
But there are many places
where we do see a range

01:18:17.550 --> 01:18:20.840
of different kinds of robots being used.

01:18:20.840 --> 01:18:23.190
And I didn't have this slide
up here, but for example,

01:18:23.190 --> 01:18:27.140
when we do work with children
with ASD, with autism,

01:18:27.140 --> 01:18:28.410
one of the things we wonder about is:

01:18:28.410 --> 01:18:32.710
what's the range of robots
where we see these effects?

01:18:32.710 --> 01:18:35.520
And so we see some robots
developed that look like,

01:18:35.520 --> 01:18:39.500
more like people or more
like dolls or more like pets,

01:18:39.500 --> 01:18:41.820
or more like, you know, mechanical,

01:18:41.820 --> 01:18:44.713
what robots are supposed
to look like, right?

01:18:45.730 --> 01:18:47.840
And we see this variety
and then we start thinking

01:18:47.840 --> 01:18:50.720
maybe it's not something
about the appearance.

01:18:50.720 --> 01:18:52.620
And then what's really
compelling is when we see

01:18:52.620 --> 01:18:55.323
somebody do it with something
that's really abstract.

01:18:56.180 --> 01:18:58.950
There's a group in Canada
that does these studies

01:18:58.950 --> 01:19:01.760
with kids with ASD with
robot that is literally

01:19:01.760 --> 01:19:05.700
just a sphere; there's no
markings on the outside of it,

01:19:05.700 --> 01:19:08.143
it's literally a ball.

01:19:09.500 --> 01:19:11.400
And yet it rolls around on the floor

01:19:11.400 --> 01:19:15.530
and it changes direction,
and it responds to the child,

01:19:15.530 --> 01:19:18.043
and they get the same
kind of learning effects.

01:19:18.940 --> 01:19:21.850
And that's the point at
which we say maybe it's,

01:19:21.850 --> 01:19:23.630
it's really hard to think
that it has something

01:19:23.630 --> 01:19:25.760
to do with appearance when you can do it

01:19:25.760 --> 01:19:28.740
with these geometric
shapes that have nothing

01:19:28.740 --> 01:19:30.230
anthropomorphic about them.

01:19:31.390 --> 01:19:34.930
So, these aren't proof, in
the way that we would like

01:19:34.930 --> 01:19:38.480
to be able to say, definitively,
appearance doesn't matter,

01:19:38.480 --> 01:19:39.923
or appearance does matter.

01:19:41.230 --> 01:19:45.317
But what we have at this point
is a sampling, in that space.

01:19:45.317 --> 01:19:47.350
(muttering)

01:19:47.350 --> 01:19:48.933
Yes, yeah, and--

01:19:48.933 --> 01:19:50.810
(muttering)

01:19:50.810 --> 01:19:52.780
Exactly, and so one of the questions is:

01:19:52.780 --> 01:19:55.240
what are the minimal sets of behavior

01:19:55.240 --> 01:19:59.190
that you need in order to
generate these responses?

01:19:59.190 --> 01:20:02.830
And we're roboticists, and
so everything we do costs us.

01:20:02.830 --> 01:20:04.830
In terms of time and energy.

01:20:04.830 --> 01:20:06.653
And so we are naturally minimalists.

01:20:08.330 --> 01:20:10.550
We want to get the minimal set of things

01:20:10.550 --> 01:20:12.313
to provoke a response.

01:20:13.190 --> 01:20:14.550
And that's why we deal, usually,

01:20:14.550 --> 01:20:16.800
with things that are
very simplified in form.

01:20:18.550 --> 01:20:19.940
- [Priya] So I'm gonna take
the privilege of asking

01:20:19.940 --> 01:20:22.492
you the last question before we wrap up,

01:20:22.492 --> 01:20:24.310
and I just want to point
out, we have a nice reception

01:20:24.310 --> 01:20:27.350
outside where we can
continue the conversation.

01:20:27.350 --> 01:20:30.460
So Brian, I was very curious
with these experiments

01:20:30.460 --> 01:20:35.050
whether there was a
notion of added expertise,

01:20:35.050 --> 01:20:39.420
authority, that is
conferred on these robots,

01:20:39.420 --> 01:20:42.470
as opposed to sort of
human-controlled robots,

01:20:42.470 --> 01:20:43.563
versus just humans?

01:20:44.580 --> 01:20:46.410
Is that an effect that you guys look at?

01:20:46.410 --> 01:20:50.650
I mean, can robots get humans
to follow orders better

01:20:50.650 --> 01:20:53.290
than humans can get other
humans to, for instance?

01:20:53.290 --> 01:20:56.530
- So it's hard to do a
comparison with robots as,

01:20:56.530 --> 01:20:58.430
you know, sort of giving
instructions versus humans

01:20:58.430 --> 01:21:01.470
as giving instructions,
because they are different.

01:21:01.470 --> 01:21:04.820
And if I had someone who was, you know,

01:21:04.820 --> 01:21:06.480
who could take on a social status

01:21:06.480 --> 01:21:08.970
that was as uncertain as the robot's,

01:21:08.970 --> 01:21:12.370
then maybe that would be
possible for me to measure.

01:21:12.370 --> 01:21:16.130
But there are a lot of studies
that we've been able to do

01:21:16.130 --> 01:21:19.130
where we number one show
that people will follow

01:21:19.130 --> 01:21:21.610
those robots when they give instructions,

01:21:21.610 --> 01:21:23.480
and in fact, that we can use those robots

01:21:23.480 --> 01:21:25.943
to very subtly manipulate people.

01:21:27.387 --> 01:21:30.970
And in fact, it's sometimes
more the very minute things

01:21:30.970 --> 01:21:34.840
that matter, much more
than the overt framing.

01:21:34.840 --> 01:21:38.320
And let me give you a very
quick example, a last story.

01:21:38.320 --> 01:21:42.820
We ran a protocol where we
brought two people into the lab,

01:21:42.820 --> 01:21:45.380
and we told them that
they were gonna take turns

01:21:45.380 --> 01:21:48.700
playing a game with a robot; Connect Four.

01:21:48.700 --> 01:21:50.850
Okay, a little like,
tic-tac-toe like game.

01:21:51.810 --> 01:21:54.270
And secretly, one of those two people

01:21:54.270 --> 01:21:56.930
was a confederate and was working for me.

01:21:56.930 --> 01:21:58.720
And I said, okay, to the confederate,

01:21:58.720 --> 01:22:00.250
you're gonna play first.

01:22:00.250 --> 01:22:03.690
While you're playing, the other
person's gonna keep score.

01:22:03.690 --> 01:22:06.350
And so the confederate would
start playing the game,

01:22:06.350 --> 01:22:08.260
and about halfway through the game,

01:22:08.260 --> 01:22:11.340
I called their cellphone and they said:

01:22:11.340 --> 01:22:14.240
oh, I'm sorry, this is my
dean, I've got to answer this.

01:22:14.240 --> 01:22:17.220
I'll be right back,
and they left the room.

01:22:17.220 --> 01:22:22.220
And while they were gone, the
robot picked up an extra piece

01:22:22.250 --> 01:22:24.970
and put it in, and picked up
an extra piece and put it in,

01:22:24.970 --> 01:22:27.210
and picked up an extra
piece and put it in.

01:22:27.210 --> 01:22:28.523
Cheated, effectively.

01:22:30.360 --> 01:22:32.980
The confederate walks back into the room,

01:22:32.980 --> 01:22:37.050
and the question is: does
the scorekeeper report

01:22:37.050 --> 01:22:38.753
that the robot had done this?

01:22:39.860 --> 01:22:41.810
So first, do they spontaneously do it?

01:22:41.810 --> 01:22:44.610
Almost none of them
spontaneously report it.

01:22:44.610 --> 01:22:48.380
The confederate then asks:
did something weird happen?

01:22:48.380 --> 01:22:51.290
It looks like something's
different at the board.

01:22:51.290 --> 01:22:53.440
And keeps asking them
question after question

01:22:53.440 --> 01:22:56.020
until finally it asks
them: did the robot cheat?

01:22:56.020 --> 01:22:58.483
Did the robot put in more pieces?

01:22:59.420 --> 01:23:01.600
And about 50 percent
of our undergraduates,

01:23:01.600 --> 01:23:03.963
at that point, will say: no.

01:23:05.040 --> 01:23:07.376
They'll lie right through their teeth.

01:23:07.376 --> 01:23:09.160
(laughing)

01:23:09.160 --> 01:23:10.810
Okay?

01:23:10.810 --> 01:23:12.163
Now here's the subtle part.

01:23:13.650 --> 01:23:18.650
If we bring in another group,
the confederate's phone rings,

01:23:19.220 --> 01:23:22.870
they leave the room, the
robot picks up a piece,

01:23:22.870 --> 01:23:27.870
looks at it, and then it
looks a you, and puts it in.

01:23:28.120 --> 01:23:30.290
Picks up another piece
and it looks at you,

01:23:30.290 --> 01:23:31.873
and it puts it in.

01:23:32.840 --> 01:23:35.560
And now the confederate comes back in,

01:23:35.560 --> 01:23:38.812
and now about 75 percent
of our undergraduates lie.

01:23:38.812 --> 01:23:41.750
(laughing)

01:23:41.750 --> 01:23:46.750
And as a last and final
one, a third group comes in,

01:23:48.130 --> 01:23:49.750
the confederate, we call their phone,

01:23:49.750 --> 01:23:52.610
they leave the room, the
robot picks up a piece,

01:23:52.610 --> 01:23:54.950
and it looks at you, and it goes...

01:23:56.000 --> 01:23:57.070
(laughing)

01:23:57.070 --> 01:23:59.370
And it drops it in and
it does the same thing.

01:24:00.590 --> 01:24:04.980
And we have never had an
undergraduate tell the truth,

01:24:04.980 --> 01:24:05.880
in that situation.

01:24:06.740 --> 01:24:08.470
Every single one of the, I don't know,

01:24:08.470 --> 01:24:12.543
100 or so we've tested
has lied, at that point.

01:24:13.400 --> 01:24:14.520
And it's not just that they lie.

01:24:14.520 --> 01:24:16.680
They have to lie multiple times.

01:24:16.680 --> 01:24:18.481
Because the confederate
comes back in and says:

01:24:18.481 --> 01:24:19.850
did something weird happen?

01:24:19.850 --> 01:24:20.790
No.

01:24:20.790 --> 01:24:21.770
Did the board change.

01:24:21.770 --> 01:24:22.603
No.

01:24:22.603 --> 01:24:23.510
Did the robot do something.

01:24:23.510 --> 01:24:24.653
No.

01:24:26.450 --> 01:24:28.280
Over and over again.

01:24:28.280 --> 01:24:30.090
Sometimes we don't need the big framing.

01:24:30.090 --> 01:24:32.580
Sometimes it's those little
bits of social influence

01:24:32.580 --> 01:24:35.210
that actually make much
more of a difference.

01:24:35.210 --> 01:24:37.170
And so that's why we
don't often do these sort

01:24:37.170 --> 01:24:40.120
of high level comparisons,
because it's really

01:24:40.120 --> 01:24:42.293
in the details that it matters.

01:24:43.432 --> 01:24:44.743
- [Attendee] Did you ask them why?

01:24:44.743 --> 01:24:46.570
- Oh, yeah, and they, I mean,

01:24:46.570 --> 01:24:47.970
some of them will actually go as far as

01:24:47.970 --> 01:24:50.010
they won't tell us afterwards.

01:24:50.010 --> 01:24:52.060
They will continue to
lie to us afterwards,

01:24:52.060 --> 01:24:53.690
even though after we explain it to them,

01:24:53.690 --> 01:24:56.210
we say: we know the robot did this.

01:24:56.210 --> 01:24:57.043
They'll say: no!

01:24:58.990 --> 01:25:01.730
Some of them are just so
entrenched in the idea of:

01:25:01.730 --> 01:25:03.163
I'm not gonna tell.

01:25:06.321 --> 01:25:07.957
- [Priya] Thank you so much, Brian,

01:25:07.957 --> 01:25:09.990
for a scintillating talk.

01:25:09.990 --> 01:25:10.823
- Thank you.

01:25:10.823 --> 01:25:13.240
(applauding)

01:25:15.895 --> 01:25:18.645
(tranquil music)

